export const PASSAGES = [{"id":"g1-u1-l01","unit":"G1-U1","pool":"listening","title":"The Missing Mitten","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"It was the first snowy day of winter. Ana put on her red coat, her boots and her warm hat. But one mitten was missing! She looked under the bed. She looked in the toy box. She even looked in the fridge. \"Mittens do not live in the fridge,\" laughed her big brother, Leo. Then their dog, Pepper, trotted in. Pepper had the red mitten in her mouth! It was a little wet and very warm. \"Pepper wanted to play in the snow too!\" said Ana. So all three of them went outside together.","questions":[{"type":"literal","q":"What was Ana looking for?","options":["her mitten","her hat","her boots"],"answer":0,"evidence":"But one mitten was missing!"},{"type":"sequence","q":"Where did Ana look first?","options":["in the toy box","in the fridge","under the bed"],"answer":2,"evidence":"She looked under the bed."},{"type":"literal","q":"Who had the mitten?","options":["Leo","Pepper","Ana"],"answer":1,"evidence":"Pepper had the red mitten in her mouth!"},{"type":"why","q":"Why did Pepper take the mitten?","options":["to keep her nose warm","to play in the snow","to hide it in her bed"],"answer":1,"evidence":"\"Pepper wanted to play in the snow too!\""}],"words":94,"auto":{"fk":1.2,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g1-u1-l02","unit":"G1-U1","pool":"listening","title":"How Bees Make Honey","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Honey bees live together in a hive. There can be thousands of bees in one hive! Worker bees fly from flower to flower. They drink a sweet juice called nectar. They carry it home in a special part of the body called a honey stomach. Inside the hive, the bees pass the nectar from bee to bee. Then they put it into little wax rooms called cells. The bees fan it with their wings to dry it out. Slowly, the nectar gets thick and sticky. Now it is honey! The bees eat it in winter, when there are no flowers.","caption":"Bees keep their honey in little wax cells.","questions":[{"type":"main","q":"What is this mostly about?","options":["how bees make honey","how bees build hives","how flowers grow"],"answer":0},{"type":"literal","q":"What do bees drink from flowers?","options":["water","milk","nectar"],"answer":2,"evidence":"They drink a sweet juice called nectar."},{"type":"why","q":"Why do bees fan the nectar with their wings?","options":["to keep it cold","to make it pink","to dry it out"],"answer":2,"evidence":"The bees fan it with their wings to dry it out."},{"type":"feature","q":"What does the caption tell you?","options":["what bees eat in spring","where bees keep honey","how bees sting"],"answer":1}],"words":100,"auto":{"fk":2.5,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g1-u1-l03","unit":"G1-U1","pool":"listening","title":"The Sandcastle Moat","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Mia and her grandpa went to the beach. They built the biggest sandcastle ever. It had four towers, a bridge, and a moat all around it. Mia stuck a shell on the top for a flag. Then a big wave rolled in. Whoosh! The water filled the moat. \"Oh no!\" said Mia. \"Our castle!\" But the castle did not fall. The moat had saved it. A tiny crab crawled out of the moat and waved a claw. \"I think he likes his new pool,\" said Grandpa. Mia laughed. They named the crab Captain Pinch.","questions":[{"type":"literal","q":"Who went to the beach with Mia?","options":["her grandpa","her sister","her dad"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Mia and her grandpa went to the beach."},{"type":"literal","q":"What did Mia use for a flag?","options":["a shell","a stick","a leaf"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Mia stuck a shell on the top for a flag."},{"type":"sequence","q":"What happened after Mia added the flag?","options":["The castle fell down.","Grandpa went home.","A big wave rolled in."],"answer":2,"evidence":"Then a big wave rolled in."},{"type":"vocab","q":"In this story, what is a moat?","options":["a kind of crab","water around a castle","a tall tower of sand"],"answer":1,"evidence":"It had four towers, a bridge, and a moat all around it."}],"words":94,"auto":{"fk":0.1,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g1-u1-l04","unit":"G1-U1","pool":"listening","title":"Why Leaves Change Color","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"In summer, most leaves are green. The green color inside a leaf helps the tree make food from sunlight. In fall, the days get shorter and cooler. The tree stops making food in its leaves. The green color fades away. Then we can see yellow and orange colors that were hiding there all along! Some leaves turn bright red, too. Soon the leaves dry up and drop to the ground. The tree rests all winter. In spring, new green leaves grow again.","caption":"In fall, leaves turn yellow, orange and red.","questions":[{"type":"main","q":"What is this mostly about?","options":["how people rake leaves","why leaves change color","why trees need rain"],"answer":1},{"type":"literal","q":"What color are most leaves in summer?","options":["green","red","yellow"],"answer":0,"evidence":"In summer, most leaves are green."},{"type":"sequence","q":"What happens after the green color fades?","options":["new green leaves grow","the tree makes more food","we see yellow and orange"],"answer":2,"evidence":"Then we can see yellow and orange colors that were hiding there all along!"},{"type":"feature","q":"What does the caption tell you?","options":["how tall trees grow","where birds sleep","leaf colors in fall"],"answer":2}],"words":82,"auto":{"fk":1.7,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g1-u1-l05","unit":"G1-U1","pool":"listening","title":"Biscuit Gets Mixed Up","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Grandma has a rooster named Biscuit. Every morning, Biscuit wakes up the whole farm. One night, Grandma put a bright new light on the porch. At midnight, Biscuit saw the light. He thought it was the sun! He crowed as loud as he could, \"Cock-a-doodle-doo!\" Grandma woke up. The cows woke up. Even the cat woke up. Grandma went outside in her slippers. She turned off the porch light. Biscuit blinked, fluffed his feathers, and went back to sleep. The next day, Grandma got a softer light. Now the whole farm sleeps all night long.","questions":[{"type":"literal","q":"What did Biscuit think the light was?","options":["the moon","the sun","a star"],"answer":1,"evidence":"He thought it was the sun!"},{"type":"sequence","q":"Who woke up first?","options":["Grandma","the cows","the cat"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Grandma woke up."},{"type":"why","q":"Why did Biscuit crow at night?","options":["he saw a bright light","he was very hungry","he heard a loud dog"],"answer":0,"evidence":"At midnight, Biscuit saw the light."},{"type":"vocab","q":"In this story, what does crowed mean?","options":["flew up very high","ate a lot of corn","called out loudly"],"answer":2,"evidence":"He crowed as loud as he could, \"Cock-a-doodle-doo!\""}],"words":95,"auto":{"fk":2,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g1-u1-l06","unit":"G1-U1","pool":"listening","title":"Octopus Tricks","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"An octopus lives in the sea. It has eight long arms and no bones at all. Because it has no bones, an octopus can squeeze through a very small hole! Each arm is covered with suckers. The suckers help the octopus grab food and even taste things. An octopus can also change color. It can turn red, brown or white to match the rocks. When an octopus is in danger, it can squirt a cloud of dark ink. Then it zooms away. The other animal cannot see it!","caption":"An octopus holds on to a rock with its suckers.","questions":[{"type":"main","q":"What is this mostly about?","options":["shark teeth","octopus tricks","crab shells"],"answer":1},{"type":"literal","q":"How many arms does an octopus have?","options":["six","eight","ten"],"answer":1,"evidence":"It has eight long arms and no bones at all."},{"type":"why","q":"Why can an octopus squeeze through tiny holes?","options":["it has no bones","it is very fast","it has dark ink"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Because it has no bones, an octopus can squeeze through a very small hole!"},{"type":"vocab","q":"What does squirt mean here?","options":["eat up slowly","hide in a hole","spray out fast"],"answer":2,"evidence":"When an octopus is in danger, it can squirt a cloud of dark ink."}],"words":88,"auto":{"fk":3.1,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g1-u1-l07","unit":"G1-U1","pool":"listening","title":"The Wobbly Tooth","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Theo had a wobbly tooth. It wiggled when he talked. It wiggled when he chewed. But it would not come out! He tried biting an apple. Nope. He tried brushing it very gently. Nope. At dinner, his little sister Rosa made a silly face. She put two noodles under her nose like a mustache. Theo laughed so hard that he sneezed. Out popped the tooth, right into his spaghetti! \"Found it!\" said Rosa. That night, Theo put the tooth under his pillow. He dreamed about noodle mustaches.","questions":[{"type":"sequence","q":"What did Theo try first?","options":["brushing the tooth","eating noodles","biting an apple"],"answer":2,"evidence":"He tried biting an apple."},{"type":"literal","q":"What did Rosa put under her nose?","options":["two carrots","two noodles","a feather"],"answer":1,"evidence":"She put two noodles under her nose like a mustache."},{"type":"why","q":"Why did Theo sneeze?","options":["he laughed so hard","he was very cold","he smelled pepper"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Theo laughed so hard that he sneezed."},{"type":"literal","q":"Where did the tooth land?","options":["in his spaghetti","on the kitchen floor","in his glass of milk"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Out popped the tooth, right into his spaghetti!"}],"words":87,"auto":{"fk":1.5,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g1-u1-l08","unit":"G1-U1","pool":"listening","title":"From Egg to Frog","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"A frog starts life as a tiny egg in a pond. Frog eggs are soft and look like clear jelly with dots inside. Soon, each egg hatches into a tadpole. A tadpole has a long tail for swimming. It breathes with gills, like a fish. As the tadpole grows, it gets back legs. Then front legs pop out. Its tail gets shorter and shorter. It grows lungs, so it can breathe air. At last, a little frog hops out of the water and onto land. One day, it may lay eggs of its own.","caption":"A tadpole slowly changes into a frog.","questions":[{"type":"main","q":"What is this mostly about?","options":["how fish catch food","why ponds dry up","how a frog grows up"],"answer":2},{"type":"sequence","q":"Which legs does a tadpole get first?","options":["front legs","back legs","all four legs"],"answer":1,"evidence":"As the tadpole grows, it gets back legs."},{"type":"literal","q":"What does a tadpole use for swimming?","options":["its back legs","a long tail","its front legs"],"answer":1,"evidence":"A tadpole has a long tail for swimming."},{"type":"feature","q":"What does the caption tell you?","options":["a tadpole changes","a frog eats bugs","a pond gets cold"],"answer":0}],"words":94,"auto":{"fk":1.6,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g1-u2-p01","unit":"G1-U2","pool":"practice","title":"Bud the Pup","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Pam has a pup. The pup is Bud. Bud can dig. He digs in the mud. He digs in a pot. Mom is mad. \"Bud, no!\" said Mom. Pam gets a tub. She puts Bud in the tub. Bud is wet. Pam is wet. Mom is wet too! Bud gets a big hug. Bud is all set. Oh no! Bud runs to the mud!","questions":[{"type":"literal","q":"What can Bud do?","options":["sit","hop","dig"],"answer":2,"evidence":"Bud can dig."},{"type":"why","q":"Why is Mom mad?","options":["Bud naps on a bed","Bud eats a hat","Bud digs in a pot"],"answer":2,"evidence":"He digs in a pot."},{"type":"sequence","q":"What does Bud do at the end?","options":["naps in a box","runs to the mud","sits in the tub"],"answer":1,"evidence":"Bud runs to the mud!"}],"words":64,"auto":{"fk":-2.3,"decodable":1,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g1-u2-p02","unit":"G1-U2","pool":"practice","title":"A Bug on a Hat","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"A red bug sat on Kim's hat. \"Look, a bug!\" said Kim. The bug did a hop. It sat on the cat. The bug did a hop. It sat on the rug. Kim got a cup. She put the cup on the rug. But the bug was not in it. Dad sat up. The bug sat on his bun! Kim got the bug. She let it go in the sun.","questions":[{"type":"sequence","q":"Where did the bug sit first?","options":["on Kim's hat","on the cat","on the rug"],"answer":0,"evidence":"A red bug sat on Kim's hat."},{"type":"literal","q":"What did Kim put on the rug?","options":["a cup","a hat","a mat"],"answer":0,"evidence":"She put the cup on the rug."},{"type":"literal","q":"Where on Dad was the bug?","options":["on his cap","on his leg","on his bun"],"answer":2,"evidence":"The bug sat on his bun!"}],"words":70,"auto":{"fk":-1.8,"decodable":1,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g1-u2-p03","unit":"G1-U2","pool":"practice","title":"The Big Box","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Dad got a big box. Tom sat in it. \"It is my bus!\" said Tom. Meg got in. The cat got in too. \"Can I get on?\" said Dad. Dad got in. Rip! The box had a big gap. Tom was sad. But Meg had a rug. She put the rug on top. It was a hut! \"A hut is good for a nap,\" said Dad.","questions":[{"type":"literal","q":"What did Tom say the box was?","options":["a van","a bus","a cab"],"answer":1,"evidence":"\"It is my bus!\""},{"type":"why","q":"Why did the box rip?","options":["the cat sat on it","Dad got in","Ben had a pen"],"answer":1,"evidence":"Dad got in."},{"type":"sequence","q":"What was the box at the end?","options":["a hut","a bed","a van"],"answer":0,"evidence":"It was a hut!"}],"words":66,"auto":{"fk":-2.2,"decodable":1,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g1-u2-p04","unit":"G1-U2","pool":"practice","title":"Sam Wants a Nap","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Sam wants a nap. He gets in his bed. His pup said, \"Yap, yap!\" Sam sat up. His hen said, \"Bok, bok!\" Sam sat up. \"Mom! I want a nap!\" said Sam. Mom got a big box. She put a rug in it. The pup got in. The hen got in. They had a nap in the box. Sam got his nap. So did the cat, on his leg!","questions":[{"type":"sequence","q":"What made Sam sit up first?","options":["his hen","his cat","his pup"],"answer":2,"evidence":"His pup said, \"Yap, yap!\""},{"type":"literal","q":"What did Mom put in the box?","options":["a cup","a hat","a rug"],"answer":2,"evidence":"She put a rug in it."},{"type":"literal","q":"Where did the pup and hen nap?","options":["on the bed","in the box","on the bus"],"answer":1,"evidence":"They had a nap in the box."}],"words":69,"auto":{"fk":-2.1,"decodable":1,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g1-u2-c01","unit":"G1-U2","pool":"check","title":"Where Is the Egg?","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Ben has a red hen. Ben wants an egg. He looks in the pen. No egg. He looks in the hut. No egg. \"Where is my egg?\" said Ben. The hen said, \"Bok!\" Dad got his cap. He put it on. Oh no! The egg was in his cap! Egg ran down Dad! Ben got a rag. \"Ben,\" said Dad, \"get a hat for the hen!\"","questions":[{"type":"literal","q":"What does Ben want?","options":["an egg","a hen","a cap"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Ben wants an egg."},{"type":"sequence","q":"Where did Ben look first?","options":["in the pen","in the hut","in the cap"],"answer":0,"evidence":"He looks in the pen."},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence tells you where the egg was?","options":["He looks in the hut.","Ben has a red hen.","The egg was in his cap!"],"answer":2,"evidence":"The egg was in his cap!"},{"type":"why","q":"Why did Ben get a rag?","options":["the hen was wet","Dad had egg on him","the pen had mud"],"answer":1,"evidence":"Egg ran down Dad!"},{"type":"main","q":"What is this story mostly about?","options":["a hen that runs","looking for an egg","a new hat for Dad"],"answer":1}],"words":66,"auto":{"fk":-2.2,"decodable":1,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g1-u2-c02","unit":"G1-U2","pool":"check","title":"Meg on the Bus","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Meg got on the bus. She sat by a man. He had a big bag on his lap. The bag did a hop! \"Is it a cat?\" said Meg. \"No,\" said the man. \"It is my pup!\" A pup got out of the bag. It sat on Meg's lap. \"Can I pet him?\" said Meg. \"Yes,\" said the man. Meg had a new pal on the bus.","questions":[{"type":"literal","q":"Where did Meg sit?","options":["by a man","by a cat","by a dog"],"answer":0,"evidence":"She sat by a man."},{"type":"literal","q":"What was on the man's lap?","options":["a red hat","a big box","a big bag"],"answer":2,"evidence":"He had a big bag on his lap."},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence tells you the bag moved?","options":["Meg got on the bus.","She sat by a man.","The bag did a hop!"],"answer":2,"evidence":"The bag did a hop!"},{"type":"sequence","q":"What happened after the pup got out?","options":["It ran off the bus.","It sat on Meg's lap.","It got in the bag."],"answer":1,"evidence":"It sat on Meg's lap."},{"type":"vocab","q":"In this story, what is a pal?","options":["a friend","a bag","a bus"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Meg had a new pal on the bus."}],"words":67,"auto":{"fk":-1.9,"decodable":1,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g1-u2-c03","unit":"G1-U2","pool":"check","title":"The New Rug","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Mom got a new rug. It is big. It is red. \"I can sit on it,\" said Mom. But the cat got on it. The cat had a nap. The dog got on it. The dog had a nap. Dad got on it. Dad had a nap! There was no rug for Mom. Mom got a mat. She sat on the mat. \"Ah,\" said Mom. \"A mat is good, too.\"","questions":[{"type":"literal","q":"What color is the new rug?","options":["red","tan","pink"],"answer":0,"evidence":"It is red."},{"type":"sequence","q":"Who got on the rug first?","options":["the dog","Dad","the cat"],"answer":2,"evidence":"But the cat got on it."},{"type":"why","q":"Why did Mom get a mat?","options":["the mat was new","no room on the rug","the cat was wet"],"answer":1,"evidence":"There was no rug for Mom."},{"type":"vocab","q":"In this story, what is a nap?","options":["a fast run","a short sleep","a good snack"],"answer":1,"evidence":"The cat had a nap."},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence tells you Dad got on the rug?","options":["Dad got on it.","Mom got a mat.","It is big."],"answer":0,"evidence":"Dad got on it."}],"words":70,"auto":{"fk":-2,"decodable":1,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g1-u2-c04","unit":"G1-U2","pool":"check","title":"The Fox Kit","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Pam saw a fox kit by a log. It was little. Its mom was not there. \"Is it sad?\" said Pam. \"Let us sit,\" said Dad. Pam sat down. Dad sat down. Up ran a big red fox. It was the kit's mom! The kit did a hop. It ran to its mom. The two ran into a den. \"The kit is not sad now,\" said Pam.","questions":[{"type":"literal","q":"Where did Pam see the fox kit?","options":["by a bus","in a box","by a log"],"answer":2,"evidence":"Pam saw a fox kit by a log."},{"type":"why","q":"Why did Pam think the kit was sad?","options":["its den had no food","it was wet from mud","its mom was not there"],"answer":2,"evidence":"Its mom was not there."},{"type":"sequence","q":"What did Pam do before the big fox came?","options":["ran to the den","sat down","got a big hug"],"answer":1,"evidence":"Pam sat down."},{"type":"literal","q":"Who ran up to the kit?","options":["the kit's mom","a big tan dog","a little red hen"],"answer":0,"evidence":"It was the kit's mom!"},{"type":"main","q":"What is this story mostly about?","options":["a kit and its mom","a kit in a box","a fox as a pet"],"answer":0}],"words":67,"auto":{"fk":-1.6,"decodable":1,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g1-u2-c05","unit":"G1-U2","pool":"check","title":"Peg the Pig","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Kim has a pig. The pig is Peg. Kim puts a wig on Peg. She puts a hat on Peg. She puts a bib on Peg. \"Peg, you look so good!\" said Kim. But Peg ran. She ran to the mud. Peg sat in the mud. The wig got mud on it. Kim did not get mad. She got in the mud too! \"Mud is fun,\" said Kim.","questions":[{"type":"sequence","q":"What did Kim put on Peg first?","options":["a hat","a bib","a wig"],"answer":2,"evidence":"Kim puts a wig on Peg."},{"type":"literal","q":"What is the pig's name?","options":["Pam","Peg","Meg"],"answer":1,"evidence":"The pig is Peg."},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence tells you where Peg ran?","options":["Kim puts a wig on Peg.","She ran to the mud.","Kim has a pig."],"answer":1,"evidence":"She ran to the mud."},{"type":"why","q":"Why did the wig get mud on it?","options":["Peg sat in the mud","Kim put it in a tub","the wig was red"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Peg sat in the mud."},{"type":"sequence","q":"What did Kim do at the end?","options":["got mad at Peg","put Peg in a tub","got in the mud too"],"answer":2,"evidence":"She got in the mud too!"}],"words":68,"auto":{"fk":-1.9,"decodable":1,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g1-u2-c06","unit":"G1-U2","pool":"check","title":"Too Hot","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"It is hot. Kim is hot. Her pup is hot. Kim sits by a fan. The pup digs a pit in the mud. Kim gets a big tub. Dad puts water in it. Kim gets in. The pup gets in. Dad gets in the tub. Oh no! The tub is too little! The water gets on the cat. The cat is not hot now. But the cat is mad!","questions":[{"type":"literal","q":"What did the pup dig?","options":["a pot","a den","a pit"],"answer":2,"evidence":"The pup digs a pit in the mud."},{"type":"sequence","q":"Who got in the tub last?","options":["the pup","Dad","Kim"],"answer":1,"evidence":"Dad gets in the tub."},{"type":"why","q":"Why is the cat mad?","options":["it got wet","it was hot","it had no nap"],"answer":0,"evidence":"The water gets on the cat."},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence tells you the tub was too small?","options":["The tub is too little!","Kim gets a big tub.","Kim sits by a fan."],"answer":0,"evidence":"The tub is too little!"},{"type":"main","q":"What is this story mostly about?","options":["a lost pup","a new fan","a hot day"],"answer":2}],"words":69,"auto":{"fk":-1.5,"decodable":1,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g1-u3-p01","unit":"G1-U3","pool":"practice","title":"The Duck and the Lunch","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Brad and his dad camp by a pond. They set up a tent. Brad has a big ham sandwich for lunch. Quack! A duck pops into the tent. It grabs the sandwich. Then it runs! Brad runs after it. The duck is quick. Splash! It hops into the pond with the sandwich. Brad is sad. Dad gets a bag of chips from the van. \"Chips for us,\" said Dad, \"and the crust for the duck.\"","questions":[{"type":"literal","q":"Where do Brad and his dad camp?","options":["by a shop","by a pond","on a hill"],"answer":1,"evidence":"Brad and his dad camp by a pond."},{"type":"sequence","q":"What does the duck do after it grabs the sandwich?","options":["it naps","it runs","it quacks"],"answer":1,"evidence":"Then it runs!"},{"type":"why","q":"Why is Brad sad?","options":["a duck got his lunch","the tent got all wet","his dad got lost"],"answer":0,"evidence":"It hops into the pond with the sandwich."}],"words":75,"auto":{"fk":-0.8,"decodable":1,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g1-u3-p02","unit":"G1-U3","pool":"practice","title":"Fran's Big Plant","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Fran has a little plant in a cup. She sets it in the sun. She gives it a drink. The plant gets big. Fran puts it in a pot. It gets so big that it fills the pot! Then a pumpkin pops up on the stem. The pumpkin gets big too. It is as big as Fran! \"What can I do with it?\" said Fran. Mom and Fran cut a big grin in it. Now the pumpkin grins at Fran!","questions":[{"type":"literal","q":"Where does Fran set the plant?","options":["in the bath","in a box","in the sun"],"answer":2,"evidence":"She sets it in the sun."},{"type":"sequence","q":"What does Fran do after the plant gets big?","options":["cuts it up","gives it to Mom","puts it in a pot"],"answer":2,"evidence":"Fran puts it in a pot."},{"type":"vocab","q":"What does grins mean in this story?","options":["yells","smiles","sits"],"answer":1,"evidence":"Now the pumpkin grins at Fran!"}],"words":80,"auto":{"fk":-0.8,"decodable":1,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g1-u3-p03","unit":"G1-U3","pool":"practice","title":"Chad and the Skunk","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Chad has a dog, Rex. Rex can sniff and sniff. Rex sniffs a skunk in the grass. \"Rex, stop!\" yells Chad. Rex did not stop. The skunk spins. Psst! Rex runs back to Chad. Rex has a bad smell. \"Yuck!\" said Chad. Chad gets the tub. He scrubs and scrubs Rex. Rex gets a bath with lots of suds. At last, Rex smells fresh. But now Chad smells bad! Then Chad gets in the tub!","questions":[{"type":"literal","q":"What does Rex find in the grass?","options":["a skunk","a frog","a cat"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Rex sniffs a skunk in the grass."},{"type":"why","q":"Why does Rex need a bath?","options":["he has a bad smell","he is in the mud","he has fleas"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Rex has a bad smell."},{"type":"sequence","q":"What happens at the end?","options":["Rex runs to the skunk","Chad gets a new dog","Chad gets in the tub"],"answer":2,"evidence":"Then Chad gets in the tub!"}],"words":75,"auto":{"fk":-2.2,"decodable":1,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g1-u3-p04","unit":"G1-U3","pool":"practice","title":"Seth's Ship","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Seth has a ship. It is a tin ship with a red flag. Seth sets it in the bath. It bobs in the bath. Then Seth adds a frog. The ship still bobs. He adds a duck, a crab and a fish. Plop! Plop! Plop! The ship sinks! Glub, glub. Seth gets his ship. He tips it. Out drips the water. He sets it back in the bath with just the frog. It bobs! \"Swim, duck!\" said Seth.","questions":[{"type":"literal","q":"What is on Seth's ship?","options":["a red cap","a red flag","a big fish"],"answer":1,"evidence":"It is a tin ship with a red flag."},{"type":"sequence","q":"What does Seth add first?","options":["a duck","a frog","a crab"],"answer":1,"evidence":"Then Seth adds a frog."},{"type":"why","q":"Why does the ship sink?","options":["too much is on it","it has a big hole","it is made of mud"],"answer":0,"evidence":"He adds a duck, a crab and a fish."}],"words":78,"auto":{"fk":-2,"decodable":1,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g1-u3-c01","unit":"G1-U3","pool":"check","title":"A Shell for Crab","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"A little crab lives in a shell. But the crab gets big. The shell is too snug. The crab must get a new shell. It tests a cup. Too big! It tests a can. Too slick! It tests a lid. Too flat! The crab is sad. At last, it spots a pink shell in the sand. It slips in. It fits! The crab digs into the sand and has a nap.","questions":[{"type":"why","q":"Why must the crab get a new shell?","options":["a big fish is there","the sand is too hot","the shell is too snug"],"answer":2,"evidence":"The shell is too snug."},{"type":"sequence","q":"What does the crab test first?","options":["a can","a lid","a cup"],"answer":2,"evidence":"It tests a cup."},{"type":"literal","q":"What color is the shell that fits?","options":["black","pink","red"],"answer":1,"evidence":"At last, it spots a pink shell in the sand."},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence tells you the new shell is good?","options":["It fits!","Too big!","But the crab gets big."],"answer":0,"evidence":"It fits!"},{"type":"vocab","q":"What does snug mean in this story?","options":["too tight","too wet","too soft"],"answer":0,"evidence":"The shell is too snug."}],"words":71,"auto":{"fk":-1.6,"decodable":1,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g1-u3-c02","unit":"G1-U3","pool":"check","title":"Brad's Drum","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Gran gets Brad a drum. Bam! Bam! Bam! Brad bangs on it a lot. It is not soft! The cat runs from the den. Dad cannot nap. Mom has a plan. She gets two socks. She puts them on the drum sticks. Tap, tap, tap. Now the drum is soft. The cat comes back. Dad naps on the bench. But then Brad sings, \"La, la, LA!\" Dad sits up. \"Can we get socks for that, too?\" he asks.","questions":[{"type":"literal","q":"Who gets Brad a drum?","options":["Mom","Dad","Gran"],"answer":2,"evidence":"Gran gets Brad a drum."},{"type":"why","q":"Why does the cat run from the den?","options":["the dog is there","the drum is loud","the den is wet"],"answer":1,"evidence":"It is not soft!"},{"type":"literal","q":"What does Mom get for the drum sticks?","options":["two hats","two socks","two mitts"],"answer":1,"evidence":"She gets two socks."},{"type":"sequence","q":"What happens after Mom adds the socks?","options":["the drum is soft","the cat runs off","Dad gets up"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Now the drum is soft."},{"type":"main","q":"What is this story mostly about?","options":["a lost cat","a big nap","a loud drum"],"answer":2}],"words":78,"auto":{"fk":-2,"decodable":1,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g1-u3-c03","unit":"G1-U3","pool":"check","title":"The Lunch Mix-Up","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Kim and Dad pack two lunch bags. Kim's lunch has a jam sandwich and a plum. Dad's lunch has a big fish sandwich. At lunch, Kim unzips her bag. It is a fish sandwich! \"Yuck,\" said Kim. At his job, Dad gets a jam sandwich and a plum. Kim's pal Fran grins. Fran gives Kim a bit of her lunch. At six, Kim tells Dad. Dad grins. \"Your jam was good,\" he said. \"Your fish was not!\" said Kim.","questions":[{"type":"literal","q":"What fruit is in Kim's lunch?","options":["an apple","a melon","a plum"],"answer":2,"evidence":"Kim's lunch has a jam sandwich and a plum."},{"type":"sequence","q":"What does Kim find when she unzips her bag?","options":["a jam sandwich","a fish sandwich","a ham bun"],"answer":1,"evidence":"It is a fish sandwich!"},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence tells you Fran helps Kim?","options":["Fran gives Kim a bit of her lunch.","Kim and Dad pack two lunch bags.","Dad grins."],"answer":0,"evidence":"Fran gives Kim a bit of her lunch."},{"type":"why","q":"Why does Kim say yuck?","options":["she got Dad's fish","she got a bad plum","she lost her bag"],"answer":0,"evidence":"\"Yuck,\" said Kim."},{"type":"main","q":"What is this story mostly about?","options":["a lost lunch bag","a trip to a shop","a lunch mix-up"],"answer":2}],"words":79,"auto":{"fk":-0.8,"decodable":1,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g1-u3-c04","unit":"G1-U3","pool":"check","title":"The Frog in Class","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Miss Pratt has a frog in a tank. The frog is Gus. At lunch, Brad spots the tank. The lid is up! Gus is not in it. \"Gus is lost!\" said Brad. The class hunts for Gus. Seth checks the desks. Fran checks the bins. Kim checks the sink. No Gus. Then Miss Pratt picks up her cup. Ribbit! Gus is in her cup! Miss Pratt grins. \"I think Gus wants a drink,\" she said.","questions":[{"type":"literal","q":"Where does Gus live?","options":["in a pond","in a tank","in a box"],"answer":1,"evidence":"Miss Pratt has a frog in a tank."},{"type":"literal","q":"Where does Seth look?","options":["the bins","the desks","the sink"],"answer":1,"evidence":"Seth checks the desks."},{"type":"sequence","q":"Where is Gus at the end?","options":["in a cup","in the sink","in a bin"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Gus is in her cup!"},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence tells you Gus got out?","options":["Kim checks the sink.","Miss Pratt grins.","The lid is up!"],"answer":2,"evidence":"The lid is up!"},{"type":"why","q":"Why does Miss Pratt say Gus got in her cup?","options":["he wants a nap","he wants a pal","he wants a drink"],"answer":2,"evidence":"\"I think Gus wants a drink,\" she said."}],"words":75,"auto":{"fk":-1.9,"decodable":1,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g1-u3-c05","unit":"G1-U3","pool":"check","title":"Chad's Big Jump","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"There is a big mud spot on the path. Chad wants to jump it. \"I can do it!\" said Chad. Chad runs fast. He jumps. Splat! Chad lands in the mud. A little frog hops up. The frog jumps the mud spot in one big hop. Chad sits up in the mud. \"How did you do that?\" he asks. The frog blinks. Chad bends his legs. He jumps! He lands on the grass! \"Thanks, frog!\" said Chad.","questions":[{"type":"literal","q":"What is on the path?","options":["a big flat rock","a big mud spot","a big log"],"answer":1,"evidence":"There is a big mud spot on the path."},{"type":"sequence","q":"What happens when Chad jumps the first time?","options":["he lands in the mud","he lands on the grass","he lands on a frog"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Chad lands in the mud."},{"type":"literal","q":"Who jumps the mud in one hop?","options":["a frog","a duck","a cat"],"answer":0,"evidence":"The frog jumps the mud spot in one big hop."},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence tells you Chad made it?","options":["Chad runs fast.","Chad lands in the mud.","He lands on the grass!"],"answer":2,"evidence":"He lands on the grass!"},{"type":"main","q":"What is this story mostly about?","options":["Chad gets a pet frog","Chad learns to jump","Chad cleans the mud"],"answer":1}],"words":77,"auto":{"fk":-2.1,"decodable":1,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g1-u3-c06","unit":"G1-U3","pool":"check","title":"The Quilt Tent","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Seth puts his quilt on the bunk bed. It is a tent! He gets in with a snack. His sis, Pam, wants in. Then the cat slips in. Then the dog jumps in. The tent is full! Then Mom asks, \"Can I come in?\" Flop! The quilt falls on them all. They are all stuck in a big quilt lump. Then they all grin. \"Best tent!\" said Pam.","questions":[{"type":"literal","q":"What does Seth use to make a tent?","options":["his rug","his quilt","a box"],"answer":1,"evidence":"Seth puts his quilt on the bunk bed."},{"type":"sequence","q":"Who wants in first after Seth?","options":["Pam","the cat","the dog"],"answer":0,"evidence":"His sis, Pam, wants in."},{"type":"sequence","q":"What happens when Mom asks to come in?","options":["the dog runs","the cat naps","the quilt falls"],"answer":2,"evidence":"The quilt falls on them all."},{"type":"vocab","q":"What does lump mean in this story?","options":["a soft bed","a tall tent","a big bump"],"answer":2,"evidence":"They are all stuck in a big quilt lump."},{"type":"main","q":"What is this story mostly about?","options":["a trip to camp","a quilt tent","a lost cat"],"answer":1}],"words":68,"auto":{"fk":-1.9,"decodable":1,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g1-u4-p01","unit":"G1-U4","pool":"practice","title":"Kate and the Kite","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Kate has a red kite. It has a white stripe. Kate and Dad go up the hill. The wind is fast. Kate lets the kite rise up and up. Then the kite dips. It lands on top of a pine! Kate is sad. She cannot get it. Dad has a plan. He gets a long pole. He pokes the kite with the pole. The kite drops into the grass. Kate grabs it. It is fine! Kate smiles. The kite rises up in the wind. What a fun time!","questions":[{"type":"literal","q":"Where does the kite land?","options":["on a pine","in the lake","on a bike"],"answer":0,"evidence":"It lands on top of a pine!"},{"type":"why","q":"Why is Kate sad?","options":["her kite is stuck","her cake fell","her bike is gone"],"answer":0,"evidence":"She cannot get it."},{"type":"sequence","q":"What happens after Dad pokes the kite?","options":["The kite drops.","The kite dips.","Kate is sad."],"answer":0,"evidence":"The kite drops into the grass."}],"words":88,"auto":{"fk":-1.2,"decodable":1,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g1-u4-p02","unit":"G1-U4","pool":"practice","title":"Mike Bakes a Cake","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Mike and Mom bake a cake. It is for Dad. Mike cracks five eggs. Mom adds milk. They bake the cake. It smells so good! Mike sets the cake on the desk. Then Rex the dog jumps up. Rex wants the cake! Mike is quick. He lifts the cake up, so Rex cannot get it. “No, Rex!” said Mike. Rex gets a bone. Dad comes home at five. He likes the cake a lot. Dad gives Mike a big hug.","questions":[{"type":"literal","q":"Who wants the cake?","options":["Rex","Mom","Dad"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Rex wants the cake!"},{"type":"why","q":"Why does Mike lift the cake up?","options":["so Rex cannot get it","so Dad can see it","so it can cool off"],"answer":0,"evidence":"He lifts the cake up, so Rex cannot get it."},{"type":"sequence","q":"What happens last?","options":["Dad hugs Mike.","Mike cracks eggs.","Rex jumps up."],"answer":0,"evidence":"Dad gives Mike a big hug."}],"words":80,"auto":{"fk":-1.8,"decodable":1,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g1-u4-p03","unit":"G1-U4","pool":"practice","title":"Jake at the Lake","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Jake and Ben ride bikes to the lake. It is hot in June. Ben jumps in the lake with a splash. Jake sits on a big stone. He cannot swim yet. He is sad. Then Ben has a plan. He gets a red tube from his bike bag. Jake slips the tube on. He hops in the lake. He kicks and kicks. He bobs up and down. Jake smiles. “This is fun!” he said. At sunset, the two pals ride home.","questions":[{"type":"literal","q":"Where does Jake sit?","options":["on a big stone","on his bike","on the dock"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Jake sits on a big stone."},{"type":"why","q":"Why is Jake sad at first?","options":["He cannot swim yet.","He lost his bike.","Ben went home."],"answer":0,"evidence":"He cannot swim yet."},{"type":"sequence","q":"What does Jake do after he gets the tube on?","options":["He hops in the lake.","He rides home.","He sits on a stone."],"answer":0,"evidence":"He hops in the lake."}],"words":81,"auto":{"fk":-1.4,"decodable":1,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g1-u4-p04","unit":"G1-U4","pool":"practice","title":"Rose and Duke","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Rose spots a pup by the gate. It has mud on its nose. The pup has a tag. The name on the tag is Duke. “Mom, Duke is lost!” said Rose. Mom picks up the pup. The tag tells them where Duke lives. Rose and Mom take Duke up the lane. They stop at a white gate. A man runs out. “Duke!” he said. He hugs the pup. “Thank you!” he said to Rose. Rose smiles a big smile.","questions":[{"type":"literal","q":"What is the pup's name?","options":["Duke","Rex","Mike"],"answer":0,"evidence":"The name on the tag is Duke."},{"type":"sequence","q":"What do Rose and Mom do after Mom picks up the pup?","options":["take Duke up the lane","give Duke a mud bath","go home to the lake"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Rose and Mom take Duke up the lane."},{"type":"main","q":"What is this story mostly about?","options":["a lost pup gets home","a pup gets a bath","Rose rides a bike"],"answer":0}],"words":79,"auto":{"fk":-1.7,"decodable":1,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g1-u4-c01","unit":"G1-U4","pool":"check","title":"The Big Slide","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Kate and Meg go to the slide by the lake. It is a big, wide slide. Kate zips down the slide. “Come on, Meg!” she said. Meg looks up at the slide. It is so big! Meg shakes. Kate has a plan. She takes Meg by the hand. “The slide is wide. We can go side by side,” said Kate. Kate and Meg sit at the top. Then they slide down, zip! They land in the sand. Meg smiles a big smile. “I want to slide five times!” she said.","questions":[{"type":"literal","q":"Who zips down the slide first?","options":["Kate","Meg","Mom"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Kate zips down the slide."},{"type":"why","q":"Why can Kate and Meg go side by side?","options":["The slide is wide.","The slide is wet.","The park is big."],"answer":0,"evidence":"The slide is wide."},{"type":"sequence","q":"What do Kate and Meg do after they sit at the top?","options":["slide down","go home","eat a cake"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Then they slide down, zip!"},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence tells you Meg likes the slide now?","options":["Meg smiles a big smile.","Meg looks up at the slide.","Meg shakes."],"answer":0,"evidence":"Meg smiles a big smile."},{"type":"vocab","q":"In this story, what does wide mean?","options":["big from side to side","tall from top to bottom","wet from the rain"],"answer":0,"evidence":"The slide is wide."}],"words":90,"auto":{"fk":-1.6,"decodable":1,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g1-u4-c02","unit":"G1-U4","pool":"check","title":"A Home on the Dune","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Brad and Dad go to the lake. Brad has a cup and a spade. They make a sand home. It has five domes and a gate. Brad puts a shell on top. Then a big wave came. Swish! The sand home is flat. Brad is sad. Dad has a plan. A dune is a big hill of sand. The waves cannot get up on the dune. Brad and Dad make a new home on the dune. Brad puts a shell on top. At dusk, they ride home.","questions":[{"type":"literal","q":"What makes the first sand home flat?","options":["a big wave","a big dog","Dad's foot"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Then a big wave came."},{"type":"why","q":"Why do they make the new home on the dune?","options":["The waves cannot get up.","The dune has big shells.","Dad likes to dig there."],"answer":0,"evidence":"The waves cannot get up on the dune."},{"type":"sequence","q":"What do Brad and Dad do last?","options":["ride home","make a sand home","go for a swim"],"answer":0,"evidence":"At dusk, they ride home."},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence tells you the first home is gone?","options":["The sand home is flat.","Brad has a cup and a spade.","It has five domes and a gate."],"answer":0,"evidence":"The sand home is flat."},{"type":"vocab","q":"In this story, what is a dune?","options":["a big hill of sand","a big wave on a lake","a red tube to swim"],"answer":0,"evidence":"A dune is a big hill of sand."}],"words":87,"auto":{"fk":-1.4,"decodable":1,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g1-u4-c03","unit":"G1-U4","pool":"check","title":"Kim and the Mule","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Kim is at camp. She gets to ride a mule. The mule is Dot. Dot is a cute mule with a white nose. Kim rides Dot up the hill. Then Dot stops. Kim pats Dot. “Go, Dot!” said Kim. Dot just stands. Kim has a plum in her bag. She gives Dot the plum. Dot likes it! Dot trots up the hill. At the top, Kim and Dot stop to rest. Kim can spot the lake from the top. What a fine ride!","questions":[{"type":"literal","q":"What kind of animal is Dot?","options":["a mule","a dog","a duck"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Dot is a cute mule with a white nose."},{"type":"why","q":"Why does Dot go up the hill again?","options":["Kim gives her a plum.","Kim pats her nose.","Kim sings a tune."],"answer":0,"evidence":"She gives Dot the plum."},{"type":"sequence","q":"What does Dot do after she gets the plum?","options":["trots up the hill","stops on the hill","naps in the sun"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Dot trots up the hill."},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence tells you Dot is stuck?","options":["Dot just stands.","Dot likes it!","Kim rides Dot up the hill."],"answer":0,"evidence":"Dot just stands."},{"type":"main","q":"What is this story mostly about?","options":["Kim gets a mule to go","Kim swims in a lake","a mule naps all day"],"answer":0}],"words":83,"auto":{"fk":-1.5,"decodable":1,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g1-u4-c04","unit":"G1-U4","pool":"check","title":"Chad and the Flute","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Chad has a white flute. He likes to play tunes on it. Today the band will play at school. Chad cannot spot his flute! It is not on his bed. It is not in his bag. Chad is upset. Then his cat, Fluff, pokes her nose out of a box. Chad looks in the box. There is the flute! Fluff had a nap on it. Chad grabs the flute and runs to school. He plays a fine tune with the band.","questions":[{"type":"literal","q":"Where is the flute?","options":["in a box","on the bed","in his bag"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Chad looks in the box."},{"type":"sequence","q":"What does Chad do last?","options":["plays a tune","naps with Fluff","looks in his bag"],"answer":0,"evidence":"He plays a fine tune with the band."},{"type":"why","q":"Why is Chad upset?","options":["He cannot spot his flute.","He has a cut on his hand.","He has to skip the band."],"answer":0,"evidence":"Chad cannot spot his flute!"},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence tells you what Fluff did on the flute?","options":["Fluff had a nap on it.","Chad looks in the box.","Chad has a white flute."],"answer":0,"evidence":"Fluff had a nap on it."},{"type":"vocab","q":"In this story, what is a tune?","options":["a song you play","a kind of cat","a box for a flute"],"answer":0,"evidence":"He likes to play tunes on it."}],"words":81,"auto":{"fk":-0.9,"decodable":1,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g1-u4-c05","unit":"G1-U4","pool":"check","title":"Pam and the Snake","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Pam spots a snake in the grass. It is a little snake. The snake has a red stripe. Pam wants to take it home. She makes it a home in a box. She puts in a stone and a stick. The snake just sits. It is not glad. “A snake has a home in the grass,” said Mom. Pam thinks. She takes the box back to the grass. She tips it. The snake slides out. It zips into its home. Pam waves. “Have fun, snake!” she said.","questions":[{"type":"literal","q":"What has a red stripe?","options":["the snake","the box","the stick"],"answer":0,"evidence":"The snake has a red stripe."},{"type":"sequence","q":"What happens after Pam tips the box?","options":["The snake slides out.","Pam takes the snake home.","Mom gets a stick."],"answer":0,"evidence":"The snake slides out."},{"type":"why","q":"Why does Pam take the box back to the grass?","options":["Its home is in the grass.","The box has a big hole.","Mom wants a box for jam."],"answer":0,"evidence":"“A snake has a home in the grass,” said Mom."},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence tells you the snake is going home?","options":["It zips into its home.","Pam spots a snake in the grass.","She puts in a stone and a stick."],"answer":0,"evidence":"It zips into its home."},{"type":"main","q":"What is this story mostly about?","options":["Pam lets a snake go home","Pam gets a pet dog","Mom digs in the grass"],"answer":0}],"words":87,"auto":{"fk":-1.5,"decodable":1,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g1-u4-c06","unit":"G1-U4","pool":"check","title":"Jake and Fran Win","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Jake and Fran are pals. Today they will play a big game. Jake gets the ball. Jake kicks it, but it zips past the net. Jake is upset. He hopes to make the next kick. Then Jake gets the ball back. He spots Fran by the net. Jake kicks the ball to Fran. Fran kicks it in the net! Jake and Fran win the game. They hop and yell. “What a fine game!” said Jake. Fran smiles. “We did it side by side!”","questions":[{"type":"literal","q":"Who is by the net?","options":["Fran","Jake","Mom"],"answer":0,"evidence":"He spots Fran by the net."},{"type":"sequence","q":"What happens first?","options":["Jake kicks it past the net.","Fran kicks it in the net.","Jake and Fran win."],"answer":0,"evidence":"Jake kicks it, but it zips past the net."},{"type":"why","q":"Why is Jake upset?","options":["His kick zips past the net.","Fran gets the ball from him.","The game has to stop."],"answer":0,"evidence":"Jake kicks it, but it zips past the net."},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence tells you who gets the ball in the net?","options":["Fran kicks it in the net!","Then Jake gets the ball back.","Jake and Fran are pals."],"answer":0,"evidence":"Fran kicks it in the net!"},{"type":"main","q":"What is this story mostly about?","options":["Jake and Fran win a game","Jake trips in the mud","Fran gets a new ball"],"answer":0}],"words":83,"auto":{"fk":-1.3,"decodable":1,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g1-u5-p01","unit":"G1-U5","pool":"practice","title":"Gail and the Rain","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Gail wakes up. Rain taps on the pane. Gail wants to play on the swing, but it rains and rains. Gail is sad. She sits by the pane and waits. Mom has a plan. “Let’s paint!” she said. Gail gets her paint and a big sheet. She paints a green tree and a gray boat. Then the rain stops. The sky is bright. Gail runs out to play. She looks up. A rainbow is in the sky! Gail paints the rainbow too.","questions":[{"type":"literal","q":"What does Gail do while it rains?","options":["She paints.","She swims.","She sleeps."],"answer":0,"evidence":"She paints a green tree and a gray boat."},{"type":"why","q":"Why does Gail stay in?","options":["It rains and rains.","She is ill.","Mom is on a train."],"answer":0,"evidence":"Gail wants to play on the swing, but it rains and rains."},{"type":"sequence","q":"What does Gail see in the sky at the end?","options":["a rainbow","a gray boat","a green tree"],"answer":0,"evidence":"A rainbow is in the sky!"}],"words":82,"auto":{"fk":-1.5,"decodable":1,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g1-u5-p02","unit":"G1-U5","pool":"practice","title":"Jay and the Boat","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Jay has a little red boat. It has a white sail. Jay and Dad take the boat to the pond. Jay sets it on the water. The wind blows. The boat sails away! It floats to the reeds. Jay cannot reach it. Dad gets a long stick. He tugs the boat back with the stick. Jay hugs his boat. Then he ties a string to it. Now the boat cannot float away. Jay and Dad play by the pond until the sun is low.","questions":[{"type":"literal","q":"Where do Jay and Dad take the boat?","options":["to the pond","to the sea","to the lake"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Jay and Dad take the boat to the pond."},{"type":"why","q":"Why does the boat sail away?","options":["The wind blows.","Dad tugs it.","Jay kicks it."],"answer":0,"evidence":"The wind blows."},{"type":"sequence","q":"What does Jay do after he hugs his boat?","options":["ties a string to it","sets it on the water","paints the sail"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Then he ties a string to it."}],"words":84,"auto":{"fk":-0.5,"decodable":1,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g1-u5-p03","unit":"G1-U5","pool":"practice","title":"Fly Away, Bee","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Kim and Gail eat toast by the window. A bee flies in! Buzz, buzz. The bee lands on the jam. Gail jumps up. “Help!” she said. Kim stays still. “The bee wants to go home,” she said. “It needs the sky and the trees.” Kim waits. The bee flies up to the window. Kim lifts the window up high. The bee zips out. Away it flies into the bright sky! Gail and Kim wave. Then they eat the rest of the toast.","questions":[{"type":"literal","q":"Where does the bee land?","options":["on the jam","on the toast","on Gail"],"answer":0,"evidence":"The bee lands on the jam."},{"type":"sequence","q":"What does Kim do after the bee flies to the window?","options":["lifts the window up","sets a cup on it","jumps up and yells"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Kim lifts the window up high."},{"type":"main","q":"What is this story mostly about?","options":["Kim and Gail help a bee","Kim and Gail bake a pie","a bee eats all the toast"],"answer":0}],"words":82,"auto":{"fk":-1.2,"decodable":1,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g1-u5-p04","unit":"G1-U5","pool":"practice","title":"Gail Grows Beans","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Gail plants three bean seeds in a pot. She wants them to grow. Each day, she checks the pot. Day one, no plant. Day two, no plant. Gail is sad. “Seeds need time,” said Dad. “They need sun and rain too.” Gail sets the pot in the sun. She gives it water each day. After a week, Gail sees a green tip! Then she sees a leaf. The plant grows and grows. At last, it has green beans! Gail and Dad eat the beans as a treat.","questions":[{"type":"literal","q":"What does Gail plant?","options":["bean seeds","grape seeds","a tree"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Gail plants three bean seeds in a pot."},{"type":"why","q":"Why does Gail set the pot in the sun?","options":["Seeds need sun.","The pot is wet.","Dad wants a nap."],"answer":0,"evidence":"They need sun and rain too."},{"type":"sequence","q":"What does Gail see first?","options":["a green tip","a leaf","green beans"],"answer":0,"evidence":"After a week, Gail sees a green tip!"}],"words":87,"auto":{"fk":-1.3,"decodable":1,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g1-u5-c01","unit":"G1-U5","pool":"check","title":"A Goat on the Road","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Jay and Mom drive to the beach. Then the van has to stop. A goat is on the road! It will not go. Mom beeps. Beep, beep! The goat just eats weeds. Jay has a plan. He has a peach in his bag. Jay hops out with Mom. He shows the goat the peach. The goat trots to Jay. Jay leads the goat off the road to a gate. The goat eats the peach. Then Jay and Mom drive on to the beach. They play in the waves all day.","questions":[{"type":"literal","q":"What is on the road?","options":["a goat","a sheep","a cow"],"answer":0,"evidence":"A goat is on the road!"},{"type":"literal","q":"What does Jay give the goat?","options":["a peach","a leaf","some weeds"],"answer":0,"evidence":"The goat eats the peach."},{"type":"sequence","q":"What happens after the goat trots to Jay?","options":["Jay leads it to a gate.","Mom beeps at it.","Jay hops out of the van."],"answer":0,"evidence":"Jay leads the goat off the road to a gate."},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence tells you the goat is stuck in one spot?","options":["It will not go.","The goat trots to Jay.","They play in the waves all day."],"answer":0,"evidence":"It will not go."},{"type":"main","q":"What is this story mostly about?","options":["a goat on the road","a van with a flat","a day at the sea"],"answer":0}],"words":90,"auto":{"fk":-1.6,"decodable":1,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g1-u5-c02","unit":"G1-U5","pool":"check","title":"Snow Day","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"It snows all night. When Kate wakes up, the street is white. “Snow day!” she yells. Kate and Seth run out to play. They pile up snow to make a snowman. He has a hat and a coat. But he has no nose! Kate looks and looks. Seth spots a pine tree. By the tree is a pine cone. “Let’s use this!” said Seth. Kate pokes the cone into the snowman. Now he has a nose! Kate and Seth sit by the snowman and eat hot toast.","questions":[{"type":"literal","q":"What does the snowman have on?","options":["a hat and a coat","a cap and a cape","a bow and a tie"],"answer":0,"evidence":"He has a hat and a coat."},{"type":"why","q":"Why does Kate look and look?","options":["The snowman needs a nose.","She wants to see a deer.","Her coat has a big hole."],"answer":0,"evidence":"But he has no nose!"},{"type":"sequence","q":"What does Seth spot after Kate looks and looks?","options":["a pine tree","a red coat","a little hat"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Seth spots a pine tree."},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence tells you the nose problem is fixed?","options":["Now he has a nose!","But he has no nose!","He has a hat and a coat."],"answer":0,"evidence":"Now he has a nose!"},{"type":"vocab","q":"In this story, what is a cone?","options":["a part of a pine tree","a hat for a snowman","a big ball of snow"],"answer":0,"evidence":"By the tree is a pine cone."}],"words":87,"auto":{"fk":-1,"decodable":1,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g1-u5-c03","unit":"G1-U5","pool":"check","title":"The Pie on the Train","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Gail and Dad ride the train to Gran’s home. Gail has a peach pie on her lap. It is a treat for Gran. The train speeds by trees and a lake. At last, the train stops. Gail and Dad hop off. Then Gail gasps. The pie is still on the seat! A man in a gray cap runs to the steps. He has the pie! “Is this your pie?” he said. Gail grins. “Thank you!” she said. Gran gets her pie, and it is still sweet.","questions":[{"type":"literal","q":"What is the treat for Gran?","options":["a peach pie","a lime cake","a bag of plums"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Gail has a peach pie on her lap."},{"type":"sequence","q":"What happens after Gail and Dad hop off?","options":["Gail gasps.","Dad naps.","Gran waves."],"answer":0,"evidence":"Then Gail gasps."},{"type":"why","q":"Why does Gail gasp?","options":["Her pie is on the train.","Her hat is on the train.","Dad is still on the train."],"answer":0,"evidence":"The pie is still on the seat!"},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence tells you who brings the pie?","options":["A man in a gray cap runs to the steps.","Gail has a peach pie on her lap.","The train speeds by trees and a lake."],"answer":0,"evidence":"A man in a gray cap runs to the steps."},{"type":"main","q":"What is this story mostly about?","options":["Gail gets her pie back","Gail bakes a pie for Dad","Dad and Gran ride a bike"],"answer":0}],"words":86,"auto":{"fk":-1.7,"decodable":1,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g1-u5-c04","unit":"G1-U5","pool":"check","title":"The Night Light","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"It is night. Jay is in bed, but he cannot sleep. The lamp is off, and it is so dim. Jay sits up. “Mom!” he said. Mom comes in. She has a little night light. It looks like a fish. Mom plugs it in by his bed. The fish glows a soft green. Now Jay can see his books and his red truck. “Thank you, Mom,” said Jay. He lies back and smiles. Jay sleeps well all night.","questions":[{"type":"literal","q":"What does the night light look like?","options":["a fish","a frog","a boat"],"answer":0,"evidence":"It looks like a fish."},{"type":"why","q":"Why does Jay call Mom?","options":["It is so dim.","He is sick.","He wants a snack."],"answer":0,"evidence":"The lamp is off, and it is so dim."},{"type":"sequence","q":"What does Mom do after she comes in?","options":["plugs it in by his bed","reads him a little book","shuts the lamp off"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Mom plugs it in by his bed."},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence tells you what the light does?","options":["The fish glows a soft green.","Jay is in bed, but he cannot sleep.","Mom plugs it in by his bed."],"answer":0,"evidence":"The fish glows a soft green."},{"type":"vocab","q":"In this story, what does dim mean?","options":["with little light","with a lot of noise","with lots of rain"],"answer":0,"evidence":"The lamp is off, and it is so dim."}],"words":78,"auto":{"fk":-1.3,"decodable":1,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g1-u5-c05","unit":"G1-U5","pool":"check","title":"The Pail and the Crab","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Jake and Rose play at the beach. They dig in the sand by the sea. Rose sets her pail by the waves. A big wave comes in. Then it slips back out. The pail floats out to sea. Rose is sad. Jake waits with her. The next wave brings the pail back. It lands on the sand by Rose’s feet. Rose grabs it. In the pail is a crab! The crab is little and green. Rose and Jake let the crab go in the sea. Then they dig a big moat.","questions":[{"type":"literal","q":"Where does Rose set her pail?","options":["by the waves","on a rock","in the van"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Rose sets her pail by the waves."},{"type":"sequence","q":"What happens after the pail floats out?","options":["A wave brings it back.","Jake swims out to it.","A crab digs a hole."],"answer":0,"evidence":"The next wave brings the pail back."},{"type":"literal","q":"What is in the pail?","options":["a crab","a fish","a shell"],"answer":0,"evidence":"In the pail is a crab!"},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence tells you the pail goes away?","options":["The pail floats out to sea.","Rose grabs it.","They dig in the sand by the sea."],"answer":0,"evidence":"The pail floats out to sea."},{"type":"main","q":"What is this story mostly about?","options":["Rose gets her pail back","Jake and Rose eat lunch","a seal swims by the sea"],"answer":0}],"words":91,"auto":{"fk":-1.3,"decodable":1,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g1-u5-c06","unit":"G1-U5","pool":"check","title":"Where Is Slim?","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Kim has a pet snail. His name is Slim. Slim lives in a tank with green leaves and a gray stone. Each day, Kim feeds Slim a leaf. One day, Kim peeks in the tank. Slim is not there! Kim looks by the stone. No snail. She looks in the leaves. No snail. Kim is sad. Then Mom sees a wet trail on the glass. Kim looks up the trail. Slim is at the top of the tank! Kim grins. She gives Slim a leaf as a treat.","questions":[{"type":"literal","q":"Where does Slim live?","options":["in a tank","in a box","in a pail"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Slim lives in a tank with green leaves and a gray stone."},{"type":"sequence","q":"Where does Kim look first?","options":["by the stone","in the leaves","on the lid"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Kim looks by the stone."},{"type":"literal","q":"What helps Kim find Slim?","options":["a wet trail","a bright light","a green leaf"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Then Mom sees a wet trail on the glass."},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence tells you where Slim is at the end?","options":["Slim is at the top of the tank!","Kim looks by the stone.","Each day, Kim feeds Slim a leaf."],"answer":0,"evidence":"Slim is at the top of the tank!"},{"type":"vocab","q":"In this story, what is a trail?","options":["a wet line left behind","a tank for a snail","a green leaf to eat"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Then Mom sees a wet trail on the glass."}],"words":88,"auto":{"fk":-1.6,"decodable":1,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g1-u6-p01","unit":"G1-U6","pool":"practice","title":"Kim and the Kite","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Kim went to the park with Dad. She had a red kite. First, Kim ran on the grass. The kite did not go up. It just hung in the air. Next, the wind came. It was a big gust! Kim held the string tight. The kite went up and up. It was high in the sky. Last, Kim and Dad sat on a bench. They had a snack and looked at the kite. \"That was fun!\" said Kim.","questions":[{"type":"literal","q":"Where did Kim go with Dad?","options":["to the park","to the pond","to the shop"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Kim went to the park with Dad."},{"type":"sequence","q":"What did Kim do first?","options":["ran on the grass","sat on a bench","had a snack"],"answer":0,"evidence":"First, Kim ran on the grass."},{"type":"why","q":"Why did the kite go up?","options":["The wind came.","Dad sat down.","Kim had a snack."],"answer":0,"evidence":"Next, the wind came."}],"words":78,"auto":{"fk":-1.6,"decodable":1,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g1-u6-p02","unit":"G1-U6","pool":"practice","title":"Ben Makes Pancakes","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Ben and Gran made pancakes at home. First, Ben cracked two eggs into a bowl. Next, Gran added milk. Ben mixed and mixed with a big whisk. Then Gran put the mix in a hot pan. Ben did not grab the pan. It was too hot! The pancakes got big and flat. Last, Ben and Gran sat down. They ate the pancakes with jam. \"These are the best pancakes!\" said Ben. Gran smiled.","questions":[{"type":"literal","q":"Who made pancakes with Ben?","options":["Gran","Mom","Dad"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Ben and Gran made pancakes at home."},{"type":"sequence","q":"What did Ben do first?","options":["cracked two eggs","added the milk","ate the jam"],"answer":0,"evidence":"First, Ben cracked two eggs into a bowl."},{"type":"sequence","q":"What did Ben and Gran do last?","options":["ate the pancakes","mixed the eggs","got a bowl"],"answer":0,"evidence":"They ate the pancakes with jam."}],"words":73,"auto":{"fk":-0.8,"decodable":1,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g1-u6-p03","unit":"G1-U6","pool":"practice","title":"Meg Plants Seeds","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Meg and Mom went out to the garden. Meg had a bag of seeds. First, Meg dug six holes in the dirt. Next, she dropped a seed into each hole. She patted the dirt on top. Then Mom gave Meg a can of water. Meg wet the dirt. Meg and Mom waited and waited. Many days passed. Last, Meg saw little green plants! She jumped and clapped. \"My seeds are growing!\" she said.","questions":[{"type":"literal","q":"Where did Meg and Mom go?","options":["to the garden","to the beach","to the shop"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Meg and Mom went out to the garden."},{"type":"sequence","q":"What did Meg do next?","options":["dropped in the seeds","jumped and clapped","got a bag"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Next, she dropped a seed into each hole."},{"type":"vocab","q":"In this story, what does patted mean?","options":["pushed down softly","dug a deep hole","ran on top of it"],"answer":0,"evidence":"She patted the dirt on top."}],"words":73,"auto":{"fk":-0.3,"decodable":1,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g1-u6-p04","unit":"G1-U6","pool":"practice","title":"Jay at the Beach","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Jay and Gail went to the beach. They had a red pail. First, they made a sand fort. The fort had three tall towers. Next, a big wave came in. Splash! The wave washed the fort away. Jay felt bad. Then Gail said, \"We can make a new fort on the dry sand.\" Last, they made a new fort far from the waves. It was big, and it stayed up all day!","questions":[{"type":"literal","q":"Who went to the beach with Jay?","options":["Gail","Kim","Dad"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Jay and Gail went to the beach."},{"type":"sequence","q":"What came next, after the sand fort?","options":["a big wave","a red pail","a new fort"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Next, a big wave came in."},{"type":"why","q":"Why did the last fort stay up?","options":["It was far from the waves.","It had a red pail.","It had three tall towers."],"answer":0,"evidence":"Last, they made a new fort far from the waves."}],"words":72,"auto":{"fk":-0.9,"decodable":1,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g1-u6-c01","unit":"G1-U6","pool":"check","title":"Chad and the Mitten","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"It was a cold day at school. Chad had red mittens. At play time, Chad went out in the snow. First, he made a big snowman with his friends. Next, Chad looked at his hands. One mitten was missing! Chad looked in the snow. He looked by the swings. Then his friend Rose ran up to him. Rose had the mitten in her hand. \"Is this your mitten?\" she asked. It was! Last, Chad put his mitten on. \"Thanks, Rose!\" he said.","questions":[{"type":"literal","q":"Where does this story happen?","options":["at school","at the beach","at a farm"],"answer":0,"evidence":"It was a cold day at school."},{"type":"sequence","q":"What did Chad do first in the snow?","options":["made a snowman","looked by the swings","put on a mitten"],"answer":0,"evidence":"First, he made a big snowman with his friends."},{"type":"literal","q":"Who found the mitten?","options":["Rose","Chad","Mom"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Rose had the mitten in her hand."},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence tells you Chad had a problem?","options":["One mitten was missing!","Chad had red mittens.","It was a cold day at school."],"answer":0,"evidence":"One mitten was missing!"},{"type":"sequence","q":"What happened last?","options":["Chad put his mitten on.","Chad made a snowman.","Chad went out in the snow."],"answer":0,"evidence":"Last, Chad put his mitten on."}],"words":82,"auto":{"fk":-0.8,"decodable":1,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g1-u6-c02","unit":"G1-U6","pool":"check","title":"Pam and the Ducks","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Pam and Dad went to the pond. Pam had a bag of oats. First, five ducks swam up to Pam. Quack! Quack! Next, Pam tossed some oats on the water. The big ducks ate them fast. Then a little duck came. It did not get any oats. Pam felt bad for it. She tossed oats just for the little duck. It ate them all up. Last, the bag had no more oats. Pam and Dad waved and went home.","questions":[{"type":"literal","q":"Where did Pam and Dad go?","options":["to the pond","to the park","to the farm"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Pam and Dad went to the pond."},{"type":"literal","q":"What did Pam have in her bag?","options":["oats","eggs","seeds"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Pam had a bag of oats."},{"type":"sequence","q":"What happened first at the pond?","options":["Five ducks swam up.","Pam waved at the ducks.","A little duck ate."],"answer":0,"evidence":"First, five ducks swam up to Pam."},{"type":"why","q":"Why did Pam toss oats just for the little duck?","options":["It did not get any.","It was a big duck.","It swam away fast."],"answer":0,"evidence":"It did not get any oats."},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence tells what Pam did at the end?","options":["Pam and Dad waved and went home.","Pam had a bag of oats.","She tossed oats just for the little duck."],"answer":0,"evidence":"Pam and Dad waved and went home."}],"words":79,"auto":{"fk":-1.1,"decodable":1,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g1-u6-c03","unit":"G1-U6","pool":"check","title":"Tom on the Farm","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Tom and Mom went to a farm. A farmer named Jake met them at the gate. First, Tom fed the hens. The hens pecked at the corn. Next, Jake let Tom help milk a cow. The milk went into a big pail. Then Tom brushed a tall brown horse. The horse was still and let Tom brush it. Last, Tom sat on the hay and ate a plum. Tom said, \"I like this farm!\"","questions":[{"type":"literal","q":"Who met Tom and Mom at the gate?","options":["Jake","Dad","Rose"],"answer":0,"evidence":"A farmer named Jake met them at the gate."},{"type":"sequence","q":"What did Tom do first?","options":["fed the hens","brushed a horse","ate a plum"],"answer":0,"evidence":"First, Tom fed the hens."},{"type":"literal","q":"What did the hens eat?","options":["corn","hay","plums"],"answer":0,"evidence":"The hens pecked at the corn."},{"type":"sequence","q":"What did Tom do after he helped milk a cow?","options":["brushed a horse","fed the hens","met the farmer"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Then Tom brushed a tall brown horse."},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence tells you Tom had fun?","options":["Tom said, \"I like this farm!\"","Tom and Mom went to a farm.","The milk went into a big pail."],"answer":0,"evidence":"Tom said, \"I like this farm!\""}],"words":74,"auto":{"fk":-0.6,"decodable":1,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g1-u6-c04","unit":"G1-U6","pool":"check","title":"Brad Makes a Car","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Brad had a big box in his yard. He wanted to make a car. First, Brad painted the box red. Next, Dad helped him make wheels. They made the wheels from paper plates. Then Brad made a steering wheel from a lid. He stuck it on the box. Last, Brad sat in his car in the yard. \"Beep! Beep!\" he yelled. Mom came out and clapped. Brad gave her a ride in his car.","questions":[{"type":"literal","q":"Where was the box?","options":["in the yard","at school","in the park"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Brad had a big box in his yard."},{"type":"sequence","q":"What did Brad do first?","options":["painted the box","made the wheels","sat in the car"],"answer":0,"evidence":"First, Brad painted the box red."},{"type":"literal","q":"What did they make the wheels from?","options":["paper plates","a big lid","a red box"],"answer":0,"evidence":"They made the wheels from paper plates."},{"type":"main","q":"What is this story mostly about?","options":["Brad makes a box car.","Brad paints his yard.","Mom gets a new car."],"answer":0},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence tells what Mom did?","options":["Mom came out and clapped.","He stuck it on the box.","First, Brad painted the box red."],"answer":0,"evidence":"Mom came out and clapped."}],"words":74,"auto":{"fk":-1.3,"decodable":1,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g1-u6-c05","unit":"G1-U6","pool":"check","title":"Rose Stays In","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"It was raining, so Rose had to stay at home. She did not want to sit and wait. First, Rose made a tent. She put a sheet on top of two chairs. Next, she got her stuffed dog and a lamp. Then she read a book in the tent with her dog. Last, the rain stopped. The sun came out! Rose put on her coat and ran out. She jumped in the wet grass.","questions":[{"type":"literal","q":"Where was Rose when it rained?","options":["at home","at school","at the pond"],"answer":0,"evidence":"It was raining, so Rose had to stay at home."},{"type":"literal","q":"What did Rose use to make a tent?","options":["a sheet and chairs","a box and plates","a lamp and a book"],"answer":0,"evidence":"She put a sheet on top of two chairs."},{"type":"sequence","q":"What did Rose do in the tent?","options":["read a book","made a snack","fed a dog"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Then she read a book in the tent with her dog."},{"type":"why","q":"Why did Rose stay at home?","options":["It was raining.","She was sick.","It was night."],"answer":0,"evidence":"It was raining, so Rose had to stay at home."},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence tells you the rain was over?","options":["Last, the rain stopped.","First, Rose made a tent.","She jumped in the wet grass."],"answer":0,"evidence":"Last, the rain stopped."}],"words":74,"auto":{"fk":-0.7,"decodable":1,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g1-u6-c06","unit":"G1-U6","pool":"check","title":"Seth Finds a Bug","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Seth and Kim went on a hike by a stream. First, Seth saw a bug on a rock. It had six legs and a green shell. \"Kim, look at this bug!\" said Seth. Next, the bug crept onto a leaf. Seth and Kim sat still and looked. Then the bug spread its wings. Zip! It went up into the trees. Last, Seth and Kim went home. Seth told Mom all about the green bug.","questions":[{"type":"literal","q":"Where did Seth and Kim hike?","options":["by a stream","at the beach","on a farm"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Seth and Kim went on a hike by a stream."},{"type":"literal","q":"Where did Seth see the bug first?","options":["on a rock","on a tree","on his hat"],"answer":0,"evidence":"First, Seth saw a bug on a rock."},{"type":"sequence","q":"What did the bug do after it went on the leaf?","options":["spread its wings","sat on a rock","hid in a shell"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Then the bug spread its wings."},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence tells what the bug looked like?","options":["It had six legs and a green shell.","Seth and Kim sat still and looked.","Last, Seth and Kim went home."],"answer":0,"evidence":"It had six legs and a green shell."},{"type":"sequence","q":"What did Seth do last?","options":["told Mom about the bug","sat on a big flat rock","looked at a green leaf"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Seth told Mom all about the green bug."}],"words":74,"auto":{"fk":-0.9,"decodable":1,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g1-u7-p01","unit":"G1-U7","pool":"practice","title":"From Egg to Frog","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","caption":"A frog sits on a log in a pond.","text":"A frog starts as an egg in a pond. The egg hatches into a tadpole. A tadpole has a tail. It swims and eats plants in the water. Next, the tadpole grows legs. Its tail gets smaller and smaller. Then it is a frog! A frog can hop on land. It can swim in the pond too. Frogs eat bugs. A frog can catch a bug fast.","questions":[{"type":"main","q":"What is this book mostly about?","options":["how a frog grows","how a duck swims","how a bug hides"],"answer":0},{"type":"literal","q":"What does a tadpole have?","options":["a tail","wings","a shell"],"answer":0,"evidence":"A tadpole has a tail."},{"type":"feature","q":"What does the caption tell you?","options":["where a frog sits","what a frog eats","how a frog hops"],"answer":0}],"words":67,"auto":{"fk":0.3,"decodable":1,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g1-u7-p02","unit":"G1-U7","pool":"practice","title":"Bees Help Plants","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","caption":"A bee sits on a pink flower.","text":"Bees live in a hive. Many bees live in one hive. Bees fly from flower to flower. They drink nectar from the flowers. Nectar is a sweet drink in a flower. Bees take the nectar back to the hive. Bees get pollen on their legs. They take the pollen from plant to plant. This helps the plants make seeds. Bees can sting, so do not grab a bee!","questions":[{"type":"literal","q":"Where do bees live?","options":["in a hive","in a pond","in a nest"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Bees live in a hive."},{"type":"why","q":"Why are bees good for plants?","options":["They help plants make seeds.","They give plants water.","They dig holes for plants."],"answer":0,"evidence":"This helps the plants make seeds."},{"type":"feature","q":"Look at the caption. Where is the bee?","options":["on a flower","in a hive","on a leaf"],"answer":0}],"words":68,"auto":{"fk":0.6,"decodable":1,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g1-u7-p03","unit":"G1-U7","pool":"practice","title":"Where Rain Comes From","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","caption":"Rain drops fall on a green leaf.","text":"Rain falls from the sky. But where does it come from? The sun heats water in lakes and seas. The water rises up into the sky. Up high, the air is cold. The water turns into little drops. The drops stick to each other and get big. When the drops get big, they fall. That is rain! Rain fills ponds and streams. Plants and animals need rain to live.","questions":[{"type":"main","q":"What is this book mostly about?","options":["how rain is made","how plants grow","how lakes freeze"],"answer":0},{"type":"sequence","q":"What happens when the drops get big?","options":["They fall.","They melt.","They float up."],"answer":0,"evidence":"When the drops get big, they fall."},{"type":"feature","q":"What does the title tell you?","options":["The book is about rain.","The book is about bees.","The book is about snow."],"answer":0}],"words":69,"auto":{"fk":0.2,"decodable":1,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g1-u7-p04","unit":"G1-U7","pool":"practice","title":"Your Teeth","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","caption":"A girl brushes her teeth.","text":"Your teeth help you eat. You use them to bite and munch. Kids get two sets of teeth. First, you get milk teeth. When you are about six, they fall out one by one. Then new teeth grow in. These teeth must last a long time. Brush your teeth two times a day. Brushing keeps them clean and strong. Sweet snacks can make holes in teeth, so eat them just once in a while.","questions":[{"type":"literal","q":"How many sets of teeth do kids get?","options":["two sets","one set","six sets"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Kids get two sets of teeth."},{"type":"main","q":"What is this book mostly about?","options":["teeth","snacks","feet"],"answer":0},{"type":"feature","q":"What is the girl in the caption doing?","options":["brushing her teeth","eating a sweet snack","losing a milk tooth"],"answer":0}],"words":74,"auto":{"fk":-0.3,"decodable":1,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g1-u7-c01","unit":"G1-U7","pool":"check","title":"All About Ducks","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","caption":"A mom duck swims with her ducklings.","text":"Ducks are birds that live near water. A duck has webbed feet. Its feet push the water, so it can swim fast. A duck has a flat bill. It uses its bill to get plants and bugs in the water. Ducks have feathers. Water rolls off the feathers, so the duck stays dry. A mom duck lays eggs in a nest. The ducklings hatch and swim with their mom.","questions":[{"type":"main","q":"What is this book mostly about?","options":["ducks","frogs","fish"],"answer":0},{"type":"literal","q":"What kind of feet does a duck have?","options":["webbed feet","big claws","long legs"],"answer":0,"evidence":"A duck has webbed feet."},{"type":"why","q":"Why does a duck stay dry?","options":["Water rolls off its feathers.","It sits in the sun all day.","It stays in its nest all day."],"answer":0,"evidence":"Water rolls off the feathers, so the duck stays dry."},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence tells what a duck uses its bill for?","options":["It uses its bill to get plants and bugs in the water.","Water rolls off the feathers, so the duck stays dry.","Its feet push the water, so it can swim fast."],"answer":0,"evidence":"It uses its bill to get plants and bugs in the water."},{"type":"feature","q":"Who is in the caption with the mom duck?","options":["her ducklings","a big frog","a little fish"],"answer":0}],"words":69,"auto":{"fk":0.4,"decodable":1,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g1-u7-c02","unit":"G1-U7","pool":"check","title":"A Seed Grows","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","caption":"A green stem pokes up from the dirt.","text":"A plant starts as a seed. Put a seed in the dirt. Give it water and sun. First, the seed gets bigger. Then it splits open. A stem pushes up out of the dirt. Next, green leaves grow on the stem. The leaves use sunlight to help the plant grow. The plant gets taller and taller. Last, the plant may get flowers. The flowers make new seeds!","questions":[{"type":"main","q":"What is this book mostly about?","options":["how a seed grows","how bees make nests","how rain falls"],"answer":0},{"type":"sequence","q":"What happens after the seed splits open?","options":["A stem pushes up.","The flowers make seeds.","The seed gets water."],"answer":0,"evidence":"A stem pushes up out of the dirt."},{"type":"literal","q":"What do the leaves use to help the plant grow?","options":["sunlight","moonlight","sand"],"answer":0,"evidence":"The leaves use sunlight to help the plant grow."},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence tells what flowers make?","options":["The flowers make new seeds!","A plant starts as a seed.","Give it water and sun."],"answer":0,"evidence":"The flowers make new seeds!"},{"type":"feature","q":"Look at the caption. What pokes up from the dirt?","options":["a green stem","a pink flower","a little bug"],"answer":0}],"words":67,"auto":{"fk":0,"decodable":1,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g1-u7-c03","unit":"G1-U7","pool":"check","title":"Fun in the Snow","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","caption":"A kid makes a snowman in the yard.","text":"Snow falls when it is very cold. Up in the sky, water freezes into snowflakes. Each snowflake has six sides. Snowflakes fall down and pile up on the land. Snow is white. It is cold and wet. When the sun comes out, snow melts. It turns back into water. Kids like to play in the snow. You can make a snowman! Put on a hat and mittens to stay warm.","questions":[{"type":"main","q":"What is this book mostly about?","options":["snow","the sun","rain"],"answer":0},{"type":"literal","q":"How many sides does a snowflake have?","options":["six","two","five"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Each snowflake has six sides."},{"type":"why","q":"Why does snow melt?","options":["The sun comes out.","Kids play in it.","It falls on the land."],"answer":0,"evidence":"When the sun comes out, snow melts."},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence tells how to stay warm?","options":["Put on a hat and mittens to stay warm.","When the sun comes out, snow melts.","Kids like to play in the snow."],"answer":0,"evidence":"Put on a hat and mittens to stay warm."},{"type":"feature","q":"What does the kid in the caption make?","options":["a snowman","a sand fort","a snowflake"],"answer":0}],"words":70,"auto":{"fk":0.5,"decodable":1,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g1-u7-c04","unit":"G1-U7","pool":"check","title":"Snails","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","caption":"A snail slides on a wet leaf.","text":"A snail has a shell on its back. The shell is its home. When it is hot and dry, a snail hides in its shell. A snail has no legs. It slides on slime. The slime helps it glide. Snails are slow. Snails eat plants and leaves. A snail has long feelers on its head. Its eyes are on the tips of the long feelers. Snails like wet days. They come out when it rains.","questions":[{"type":"main","q":"What is this book mostly about?","options":["snails","frogs","bees"],"answer":0},{"type":"literal","q":"Where are a snail's eyes?","options":["on its feelers","on its shell","on its back"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Its eyes are on the tips of the long feelers."},{"type":"why","q":"Why does a snail hide in its shell?","options":["It is hot and dry.","It wants to eat.","It is a wet day."],"answer":0,"evidence":"When it is hot and dry, a snail hides in its shell."},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence tells how a snail moves?","options":["It slides on slime.","Snails eat plants and leaves.","The shell is its home."],"answer":0,"evidence":"It slides on slime."},{"type":"feature","q":"Look at the caption. Where is the snail?","options":["on a wet leaf","in a pond","on a big rock"],"answer":0}],"words":75,"auto":{"fk":-0.9,"decodable":1,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g1-u7-c05","unit":"G1-U7","pool":"check","title":"Birds and Nests","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","caption":"A bird brings a bug to its chicks.","text":"All birds have feathers and wings. Most birds can fly. A bird has a beak. It has no teeth. It uses its beak to pick up seeds and bugs. Birds lay eggs. Many birds make nests for the eggs. They use twigs, grass and mud. A bird sits on its eggs to keep them warm. Then the chicks hatch. The big birds bring bugs to the chicks. After a while, the chicks can fly.","questions":[{"type":"main","q":"What is this book mostly about?","options":["birds","bugs","fish"],"answer":0},{"type":"literal","q":"What do birds use to make nests?","options":["twigs, grass and mud","sand, rocks and shells","paper and plates"],"answer":0,"evidence":"They use twigs, grass and mud."},{"type":"why","q":"Why does a bird sit on its eggs?","options":["to keep them warm","to make them big","to hide the nest"],"answer":0,"evidence":"A bird sits on its eggs to keep them warm."},{"type":"sequence","q":"What happens after the chicks hatch?","options":["The big birds bring bugs.","The bird lays eggs.","The bird makes a nest."],"answer":0,"evidence":"The big birds bring bugs to the chicks."},{"type":"feature","q":"What does the bird in the caption bring?","options":["a bug","a twig","an egg"],"answer":0}],"words":74,"auto":{"fk":-0.7,"decodable":1,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g1-u7-c06","unit":"G1-U7","pool":"check","title":"The Sun","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","caption":"The sun sets over the hills.","text":"The sun is a star. It is the star that is the closest to us. The sun is very big and very hot. It gives us light and heat. Plants need sunlight to grow. Each day, the sun comes up in the east. It sets in the west at night. Then the sky gets dark. Do not look at the sun. It can hurt your eyes. On hot days, put on a hat.","questions":[{"type":"main","q":"What is this book mostly about?","options":["the sun","the rain","the hills"],"answer":0},{"type":"literal","q":"What is the sun?","options":["a star","a hill","a plant"],"answer":0,"evidence":"The sun is a star."},{"type":"literal","q":"Where does the sun come up each day?","options":["in the east","in the west","in the hills"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Each day, the sun comes up in the east."},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence tells how the sun can hurt you?","options":["It can hurt your eyes.","The sun is a star.","Then the sky gets dark."],"answer":0,"evidence":"It can hurt your eyes."},{"type":"feature","q":"What does the caption show the sun doing?","options":["setting over the hills","coming up in the east","shining on a plant"],"answer":0}],"words":73,"auto":{"fk":-0.4,"decodable":1,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"pl-k-01","pool":"placement-k","title":"The Red Hen","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Pam has a red hen. The hen sits in a box. It is a big box. The hen gets up. Look! The hen has an egg. Pam pets the hen. The hen is a good hen.","questions":[{"type":"literal","q":"Who has a red hen?","options":["Pam","Ben","Kim"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Pam has a red hen."},{"type":"literal","q":"Where does the hen sit?","options":["in a box","on a bed","in a cup"],"answer":0,"evidence":"The hen sits in a box."},{"type":"sequence","q":"What does the hen have when it gets up?","options":["an egg","a hat","a bug"],"answer":0,"evidence":"The hen has an egg."}],"words":36,"auto":{"fk":-2,"decodable":1,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"pl-k-02","pool":"placement-k","title":"Ben and the Pup","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Ben has a tan pup. The pup can dig. It digs in the mud. Mud is on his legs! Ben gets a tub. The pup sits in the tub. Now the pup is not a mud pup. Ben hugs his pup.","questions":[{"type":"literal","q":"What can the pup do?","options":["dig","run","hop"],"answer":0,"evidence":"The pup can dig."},{"type":"sequence","q":"What does Ben get when the pup is muddy?","options":["a tub","a mop","a rug"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Ben gets a tub."},{"type":"literal","q":"Where does the pup sit?","options":["in the tub","in the mud","on the bed"],"answer":0,"evidence":"The pup sits in the tub."}],"words":41,"auto":{"fk":-1.8,"decodable":1,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"pl-k-03","pool":"placement-k","title":"Kim and the Hat","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Kim has a red hat. Kim sets the hat on a log. A cat gets in the hat! Kim is sad. The cat naps and naps. At last, the cat gets up. Kim gets the hat. Kim pats the cat.","questions":[{"type":"literal","q":"Where does Kim set the hat?","options":["on a log","on a bed","in a box"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Kim sets the hat on a log."},{"type":"literal","q":"What gets in the hat?","options":["a cat","a pig","a dog"],"answer":0,"evidence":"A cat gets in the hat!"},{"type":"why","q":"Why is Kim sad?","options":["a cat is in her hat","her hat is wet","her hat has a rip"],"answer":0,"evidence":"A cat gets in the hat!"}],"words":40,"auto":{"fk":-1.8,"decodable":0.95,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"pl-g1-01","pool":"placement-g1","title":"Jay and the Kite","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Jay has a new kite. It is red and green. Jay and Dad go up a big hill. The wind is strong today. Jay runs fast. The kite goes up, up, up! It flies high in the sky. Then the wind stops. The kite falls down into a tree. Jay is sad. Dad gets a long stick. He pokes the kite free. Jay smiles. \"Let us try again,\" said Dad.","questions":[{"type":"literal","q":"What colors is Jay's kite?","options":["red and green","blue and pink","black and gold"],"answer":0,"evidence":"It is red and green."},{"type":"sequence","q":"Where does the kite land when the wind stops?","options":["in a tree","in a lake","on a roof"],"answer":0,"evidence":"The kite falls down into a tree."},{"type":"why","q":"Why does the kite fall?","options":["the wind stops","Jay lets it go","the string breaks"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Then the wind stops."},{"type":"main","q":"What is the story mostly about?","options":["Jay flies a kite with Dad","Jay climbs a tall tree","Dad buys a new kite"],"answer":0}],"words":70,"auto":{"fk":-1.2,"decodable":1,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"pl-g1-02","pool":"placement-g1","title":"Busy Bees","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Bees are small bugs with six legs. Many bees live in a hive. A hive can be inside a tree. Bees fly from plant to plant. They sip a sweet drink from each blossom. Then they take it back to the hive. In the hive, bees make it into honey. Bees can sting, so keep your hands still. The bee may fly away. Bees help plants make seeds. We need bees!","caption":"A bee sits on a pink blossom.","questions":[{"type":"literal","q":"Where do many bees live?","options":["in a hive","in a pond","in a cave"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Many bees live in a hive."},{"type":"why","q":"Why do we need bees?","options":["they help plants make seeds","they help the rain to fall","they keep the bugs away"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Bees help plants make seeds."},{"type":"feature","q":"What does the caption show?","options":["a bee on a blossom","a bee in a hive","a bug on a leaf"],"answer":0},{"type":"main","q":"What is this mostly about?","options":["how bees live and help","how to plant a seed","why bugs have six legs"],"answer":0}],"words":71,"auto":{"fk":-0.3,"decodable":0.99,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"pl-g1-03","pool":"placement-g1","title":"Gail and the Boat","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Gail has a little boat. Gail and Mom made it from a box and a stick. Gail and Mom go to the creek. Gail sets the boat on the water. It floats! Then the wind blows. The boat floats away from Gail. \"Oh no!\" said Gail. Mom runs on the bank. She holds out a long stick. She pulls the boat back to the bank. Gail hugs Mom. \"Next time, I will hold the string,\" said Gail.","questions":[{"type":"literal","q":"Where do Gail and Mom take the boat?","options":["to the creek","to the lake","to the beach"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Gail and Mom go to the creek."},{"type":"why","q":"Why does the boat float away?","options":["the wind blows","Gail pushes it","a duck tugs it"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Then the wind blows."},{"type":"sequence","q":"What does Mom do after she runs on the bank?","options":["holds out a long stick","jumps into the water","calls for help"],"answer":0,"evidence":"She holds out a long stick."},{"type":"vocab","q":"In the story, what does floats mean?","options":["stays on top of water","sinks to the bottom","flies in the air"],"answer":0,"evidence":"It floats!"}],"words":77,"auto":{"fk":-1.2,"decodable":1,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"pl-g1-04","pool":"placement-g1","title":"Snow Day","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Snow falls all night. When Kim wakes up, the grass is white. Kim and Seth go out to play. They roll the snow into three big balls. They stack the balls up high. Now they have a snowman! Seth gets two sticks for hands. Kim finds a red hat. The snowman needs a nose. Seth gives it a green bean. Then the sun comes out. Drip, drip, drip! The snowman gets thin. \"We can make a new one next time,\" said Kim.","questions":[{"type":"sequence","q":"What happens first in the story?","options":["snow falls all night","the sun comes out","Kim finds a red hat"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Snow falls all night."},{"type":"literal","q":"What does Seth use for the nose?","options":["a green bean","a red hat","two sticks"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Seth gives it a green bean."},{"type":"why","q":"Why does the snowman get thin?","options":["the sun comes out","the wind blows","Kim takes the hat"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Then the sun comes out."},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence tells you the snow is melting?","options":["Drip, drip, drip!","Kim finds a red hat.","They stack the balls up high."],"answer":0,"evidence":"Drip, drip, drip!"}],"words":82,"auto":{"fk":-0.9,"decodable":1,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"pl-g2-01","pool":"placement-g2","title":"The Squirrel and the Rabbit","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Late in the fall, a busy squirrel named Pip gathered acorns. She hid them under the roots of a tall oak tree. Her friend Rabbit hopped past and grinned. \"Why are you working so hard?\" he asked. \"The sun is warm, and there is plenty of food.\" Pip kept digging and storing her acorns. Soon the first snow came. The ground turned white and hard, and Rabbit could not find a single green leaf. He shivered beside the oak tree, hungry and cold. Pip peeked out of her nest and saw him. She did not tease him. Instead, she shared her acorns and showed him where to dig for roots. The next fall, Rabbit gathered food early, right beside his friend. Lesson: Getting ready early helps you, and sharing helps everyone.","questions":[{"type":"why","q":"Why did Rabbit go hungry in the winter?","options":["he saved no food in fall","Pip took all of his food","he was too sleepy to eat"],"answer":0,"evidence":"The ground turned white and hard, and Rabbit could not find a single green leaf."},{"type":"sequence","q":"What did Pip do when she saw Rabbit in the snow?","options":["shared her acorns","laughed at him","hid in her nest"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Instead, she shared her acorns and showed him where to dig for roots."},{"type":"vocab","q":"In the story, what does gathered mean?","options":["collected","threw away","planted"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Late in the fall, a busy squirrel named Pip gathered acorns."},{"type":"main","q":"What lesson does the fable teach?","options":["Get ready early and share.","Rabbits are faster than squirrels.","Always sleep in the winter."],"answer":0}],"words":131,"auto":{"fk":2.2,"decodable":1,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"pl-g2-02","pool":"placement-g2","title":"From Egg to Frog","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"A frog does not start out looking like a frog. It begins as a tiny egg in a pond. A mother frog can lay hundreds of eggs in one soft, jelly clump. After one to three weeks, a tadpole wiggles out of each egg. A tadpole has a long tail and breathes with gills, the way a fish does. It swims around and nibbles tiny plants under the water. Little by little, the tadpole changes. Its back legs grow first, and then its front legs appear. Its tail gets shorter and shorter. Inside its body, lungs form so it can breathe air. After about three months, the young frog hops out onto land. Someday it may lay eggs of its own, and the cycle will begin again.","caption":"A tadpole with new back legs swims in a pond.","questions":[{"type":"feature","q":"What does the caption tell about the picture?","options":["a tadpole with back legs","a frog sitting on land","eggs in a jelly clump"],"answer":0},{"type":"sequence","q":"What grows first on a tadpole?","options":["back legs","its lungs","big eyes"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Its back legs grow first, and then its front legs appear."},{"type":"literal","q":"How does a tadpole breathe under water?","options":["with gills","with lungs","with its tail"],"answer":0,"evidence":"A tadpole has a long tail and breathes with gills, the way a fish does."},{"type":"main","q":"What is this mostly about?","options":["how a frog grows up","how fish swim in ponds","what frogs like to eat"],"answer":0}],"words":127,"auto":{"fk":3.5,"decodable":1,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"pl-g2-03","pool":"placement-g2","title":"The Missing Library Book","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Rosa's library book was due on Friday, but on Thursday night she could not find it anywhere. She searched under her bed and behind the couch. She even checked inside the refrigerator, because once she had left her lunchbox there. Her little brother, Mateo, followed her from room to room. \"Is it red with a dragon on the cover?\" he asked. Rosa spun around. \"Yes! Have you seen it?\" Mateo nodded and led her to the dog's basket. There was the book, tucked under a blanket beside a sleepy puppy named Pickles. \"I was reading him a bedtime story,\" Mateo explained. Rosa giggled and hugged him. The next morning, she returned the book on time, and she promised to read Mateo a story every night.","questions":[{"type":"literal","q":"Where did Rosa find the book?","options":["in the dog's basket","under her bed","behind the couch"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Mateo nodded and led her to the dog's basket."},{"type":"why","q":"Why did Rosa look in the refrigerator?","options":["she once left her lunch there","she wanted a snack after school","her mom told her to look there"],"answer":0,"evidence":"She even checked inside the refrigerator, because once she had left her lunchbox there."},{"type":"vocab","q":"In the story, what does due mean?","options":["must be returned","is very heavy","is lost"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Rosa's library book was due on Friday, but on Thursday night she could not find it anywhere."},{"type":"sequence","q":"What did Rosa do the next morning?","options":["returned the book on time","read a story to Pickles","searched behind the couch"],"answer":0,"evidence":"The next morning, she returned the book on time, and she promised to read Mateo a story every night."}],"words":125,"auto":{"fk":4,"decodable":0.98,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"pl-g2-04","pool":"placement-g2","title":"Why Leaves Change Color","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Leaves are green in summer because they hold a green coloring. This coloring helps them make food from sunlight. In the fall, the days get shorter and cooler. Many trees stop making food and get ready to rest for the winter. As the green coloring fades, other colors that were hiding in the leaf begin to show. Yellow and orange were there all summer long! Some leaves also make new red and purple colors in the fall. Later, the leaves turn brown, dry up, and drop to the ground. The tree is not sick. It is saving water and energy until spring, when new green leaves will grow.","caption":"Red, orange, and yellow maple leaves in the fall.","questions":[{"type":"why","q":"Why are leaves green in the summer?","options":["they hold a green coloring","they drink lots of rainwater","they are painted by the sun"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Leaves are green in summer because they hold a green coloring."},{"type":"sequence","q":"What happens to leaves after they turn brown?","options":["they dry up and drop","they turn green again","they grow bigger"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Later, the leaves turn brown, dry up, and drop to the ground."},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence tells you that yellow was in the leaf all along?","options":["Yellow and orange were there all summer long!","Some leaves also make new red and purple colors in the fall.","The tree is not sick."],"answer":0,"evidence":"Yellow and orange were there all summer long!"},{"type":"feature","q":"What does the caption tell you?","options":["leaves on a maple in fall","green leaves in the spring","a bare tree in the snow"],"answer":0}],"words":108,"auto":{"fk":3.8,"decodable":0.99,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"pl-listen-01","pool":"placement-listen","title":"Saturday Pancakes","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Every Saturday, Nia and her grandpa make pancakes together. Grandpa cracks the eggs, and Nia stirs the batter with a big wooden spoon. This Saturday, Grandpa opened the fridge and frowned. \"Oh dear, we are out of milk,\" he said. Nia had an idea. She knocked on the door next door, where Miss Lee lived. Miss Lee smiled and handed Nia a carton of milk. Soon the kitchen smelled sweet and buttery. Nia carried a warm stack of pancakes back to Miss Lee to say thank you. \"Now that is a good neighbor,\" Grandpa said with a wink.","questions":[{"type":"literal","q":"What were Nia and Grandpa out of?","options":["milk","eggs","butter"],"answer":0,"evidence":"\"Oh dear, we are out of milk,\" he said."},{"type":"sequence","q":"What did Nia do after Grandpa found no milk?","options":["knocked on Miss Lee's door","stirred the pancake batter","called the store for milk"],"answer":0,"evidence":"She knocked on the door next door, where Miss Lee lived."},{"type":"vocab","q":"What does neighbor mean in this story?","options":["a person who lives close by","a cook who makes breakfast","a spoon for stirring batter"],"answer":0,"evidence":"She knocked on the door next door, where Miss Lee lived."},{"type":"why","q":"Why did Nia bring pancakes to Miss Lee?","options":["to say thank you","to sell them","to get more eggs"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Nia carried a warm stack of pancakes back to Miss Lee to say thank you."}],"words":98,"auto":{"fk":2.9,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"pl-listen-02","pool":"placement-listen","title":"How Penguins Stay Warm","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Emperor penguins live in Antarctica. It is one of the coldest places on Earth. The wind can howl, and the air is colder than a freezer. So how do these birds stay warm? First, a thick layer of blubber under their skin works like a cozy blanket. Next, their short feathers are packed close together. They keep out wind and water. On the coldest days, hundreds of penguins huddle in a big group. They take turns in the warm middle, so everyone gets a chance to warm up. A penguin dad even keeps his egg warm on top of his feet!","caption":"Emperor penguins huddle close together on the ice.","questions":[{"type":"main","q":"What is this mostly about?","options":["how penguins stay warm","how penguins catch fish","where penguins build nests"],"answer":0},{"type":"literal","q":"What works like a blanket for a penguin?","options":["a layer of blubber","a pile of soft snow","a nest of grass"],"answer":0,"evidence":"First, a thick layer of blubber under their skin works like a cozy blanket."},{"type":"why","q":"Why do penguins take turns in the middle?","options":["so everyone gets warm","to find more food","to hide from birds"],"answer":0,"evidence":"They take turns in the warm middle, so everyone gets a chance to warm up."},{"type":"literal","q":"Where does a penguin dad keep his egg?","options":["on top of his feet","inside a deep hole","under a big rock"],"answer":0,"evidence":"A penguin dad even keeps his egg warm on top of his feet!"}],"words":101,"auto":{"fk":3.3,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"pl-listen-03","pool":"placement-listen","title":"The Missing Mitten","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"On the first snowy day of winter, Leo could only find one blue mitten. He looked in his coat pockets, under the bench by the door, and even inside his boots. No mitten. \"We will be late for the bus,\" Mom called. Just then, Leo heard giggling from the living room. His little sister, June, was sitting behind the sofa. The blue mitten was on her hand, and she had drawn a silly face on it with a marker. \"It's a puppet!\" she said proudly. Leo smiled. He gave June his old red sock for a new puppet, and he wore both mittens to school.","questions":[{"type":"literal","q":"What was Leo looking for?","options":["his blue mitten","his red sock","his school bus"],"answer":0,"evidence":"On the first snowy day of winter, Leo could only find one blue mitten."},{"type":"sequence","q":"What did Leo hear after Mom called?","options":["giggling","a barking dog","the bus horn"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Just then, Leo heard giggling from the living room."},{"type":"why","q":"Why was the mitten on June's hand?","options":["she made it a puppet","her hands were cold","she was going outside"],"answer":0,"evidence":"\"It's a puppet!\""},{"type":"vocab","q":"What does proudly mean in this story?","options":["pleased with what she made","worried she was in trouble","sleepy and ready for bed"],"answer":0,"evidence":"\"It's a puppet!\""}],"words":105,"auto":{"fk":2.5,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"oral-01","pool":"oral","title":"The Stuck Truck","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Max has a big red truck. He fills the truck with sand. He pushes it up a hill. Then the truck gets stuck in a hole. Max pulls and pulls. It will not move. His sister Gail comes to help. She digs the sand away from the wheels. Max pushes, and the truck rolls free. \"We make a good team,\" said Max. Gail grins. They play in the sand until it is time for lunch.","questions":[],"words":75,"auto":{"fk":-0.7,"decodable":1,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"oral-02","pool":"oral","title":"Snails","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"A snail has a shell on its back. The shell is its home. When it is hot and dry, the snail hides inside. A snail has one big foot. It slides on a trail of slime. The slime helps it glide over rocks and sticks. Snails have eyes at the tips of long feelers. Snails munch on plants and leaves at night. They like wet days best. Next time it rains, look for a snail!","questions":[],"words":75,"auto":{"fk":-0.2,"decodable":0.99,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"oral-03","pool":"oral","title":"Kate Bakes Bread","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Kate and Dad bake bread on Sunday. First, they mix flour and water in a big dish. Dad adds a pinch of salt. Kate presses and rolls the bread with her hands. Then they let it rest in a warm spot. The bread puffs up! Dad puts it in the hot oven. Soon the home smells so good. Kate waits and waits. At last, the bread is done. Kate gets the first bite. \"Yum!\" she says.","questions":[],"words":76,"auto":{"fk":-1,"decodable":0.97,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"oral-04","pool":"oral","title":"Where Rain Comes From","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Where does rain come from? The sun heats the water in lakes and seas. Some of the water rises up into the sky as a gas we can not see. High up, the air is cold. The gas turns into small drops of water. Lots of drops join to make clouds. When the drops get big, they fall as rain. The rain fills the lakes and seas. Then it all starts again.","questions":[],"words":72,"auto":{"fk":0.3,"decodable":0.97,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"oral-05","pool":"oral","title":"Jay Grows Beans","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Jay plants beans in a pot. Each day, she gives them water. She sets the pot in the sun. At first, nothing happens. Jay waits and waits. Then, one day, a green stem pokes up! Soon the stem has leaves. It gets tall. It climbs up a stick. Pods hang from the vine. At last, Jay picks a bunch of beans. She shares them with Mom and Dad at dinner.","questions":[],"words":70,"auto":{"fk":-0.8,"decodable":0.97,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"oral-06","pool":"oral","title":"Owls at Night","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Owls are birds that hunt at night. They have big eyes to help them see in the dark. An owl can turn its head far to look behind it. Its soft feathers make no sound when it flies. Owls can hear well, too. An owl can hear a bug in the grass. Owls eat bugs, frogs and mice. In the day, owls rest in trees or barns. They sleep until the sun sets.","questions":[],"words":73,"auto":{"fk":0,"decodable":0.99,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"oral-07","pool":"oral","title":"Ben Swims","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Ben is at the lake with Mom. He wants to swim, but the water looks deep. Mom holds his hands. \"Kick, kick, kick!\" she says. Ben kicks his legs. Then he floats on his back. He looks up at the sky. A duck swims by. Ben grins. \"I am swimming like a duck!\" he yells. Mom claps. \"Next time, I will swim by myself,\" Ben says.","questions":[],"words":66,"auto":{"fk":-1.4,"decodable":1,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"oral-08","pool":"oral","title":"Ants Work Together","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Ants are small bugs that live in big groups. Lots of ants live in one nest in the dirt. Each nest has a queen. The queen lays the eggs. The other ants are workers. Some dig tunnels. Some get food and bring it back. Ants are very strong. An ant can lift things that weigh more than it does. Ants leave a smell on the path to food. Then the other ants can find it.","questions":[],"words":75,"auto":{"fk":0,"decodable":0.95,"checks":"pass"}}];
